\subsection{Applications and examples}\label{sect:app}

\subsubsection{ Symplectic manifolds with $k$-points blow-up}

The process of blowing up in complex geometry is well-known. Blowing up replaces a point $x$ in a complex manifold $X$ by the set $\Sigma$ of all lines through this point, which is called an \textit{exceptional divisor} biholomorphic to $\C P^{n-1}$. In the symplectic setting, the role of a point is played by a symplectically embedded standard ball, and blowing up amounts to removing the interior of a symplectic ball and collapsing the bounding sphere via
the Hopf map to the exceptional divisor.

Let $(M,\omega)$ be a closed symplectic manifold with   minimal symplectic period $p>0$ which admits a nonzero Gromov-Witten invariant of the form~(\ref{e:blowup}). Recall that the \textit{Gromov width} $c_G$ of a symplectic manifold $(M^{2n},\omega)$ is defined as
\[
c_G:=\sup\{\pi r^2|  \exists\;\hbox{a symplectic embedding}\;B^{2n}(r)\hookrightarrow M\}
\] 
where $B^{2n}(r)$ stands for the open ball of radius $r>0$ with center at the origin in $\R^{2n}$. It is well known that $c_G\leq \Theta(M,\omega)$, see~\cite{Gr,Lu1,Lu2}. 

Given $k,m\in\N$ with $\frac{kp}{m}< c_G$, 
we now take a symplectic embedding $\psi: B^{2n}(\lambda)\hookrightarrow M$ such that $\pi\lambda^2=\frac{kp}{m}$ and a positive number $\epsilon\in\R$ such that $\pi(\lambda+\epsilon)^2<c_G$. Associated to $\psi$, we blow up $(M,\omega)$ at some point $q_0\in M$ of weight $\lambda$, and denote by $(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{\omega}_{\psi,\lambda,\epsilon})$ the resulting symplectic manifold. For the details about this symplectic surgery, we refer the readers to McDuff-Salamon~\cite[Section~7.1]{MS0} and the references therein. We abbreviate $\widetilde{\omega}=\widetilde{\omega}_{\psi,\lambda,\epsilon}$. We denote by $Z\subset\widetilde{M}$ the exceptional divisor which can be identified with $\C P^{n-1}$, and $\pi_M:\widetilde{M}\to M$ the projection map given by $\pi_M|_{M\setminus\{q_0\}}=id$ and $\pi_M(\widetilde{q})=q_0$ for all $\widetilde{q}\in Z$. For simplicity, for any cohomology classes $a\in H^*(M;\Q)$, $b\in H^*(M;\Z)$ we denote the pulling back classes $a\in H^*(\widetilde{M};\Q)$, $b\in H^*(\widetilde{M};\Z)$ as 
\[
\widetilde{a}=\pi_M^*a,\quad \widetilde{b}=\pi_M^*b.
\]
Let $e$ denote the Poincar\'{e} dual of the divisor $Z$ in $H^2(\widetilde{M};\Z)$, i.e., $e={PD}_{\widetilde{M}}[Z]$. It is well known that 
\begin{equation}\label{e:omegac1}
[\widetilde{\omega}]=\widetilde{[\omega]}-\pi\lambda^2e,\quad 
c_1(T\widetilde{M})=\widetilde{c_1(TM)}-(n-1)e
\end{equation}
(see page~311 in~\cite{MS0}). 
From (\ref{e:omegac1}) and the fact that $\widetilde{M}$ is diffeomorphic to the oriented connected sum $M\sharp\overline{\C P^n}$ (here $\overline{\C P^n}$ is the manifold $\C P^n$ with the opposite orientation), it is not hard to see that the symplectic period of $(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{\omega})$ is $p\cdot{\rm gcd}(m,k)/{m}$, and that $(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{\omega})$ is monotone if $(M,\omega)$ is monotone with $\omega|_{\pi_2(M)}=\kappa c_1(TM)|_{\pi_2(M)}$ and $\pi\lambda^2=\kappa(n-1)$. 

According to the existence of a nonzero Gromov-Witten invariant of the form~(\ref{e:blowup}), one may assume that $A\in H_2(M;\Z)/{\rm torsion}$ is a class which realizes the quantity $\Theta(M,\omega)$, see~Remark~\ref{rmk:mainthm}. Hence, we get a nonzero Gromov-Witten invariant of the form:
\begin{equation}\notag
{\rm GW}^M_{0,k+3,A}\big([pt],a_0,[pt],a_1\ldots,a_k\big)
\end{equation}
with $\Theta(M,\omega)=\langle[\omega],A\rangle$,  where $a_0,\ldots,a_k$ are rational homology classes of even degree. Hu~\cite{Hu} obtained an explicit relation of Gromov-Witten invariants between a symplectic manifold and its one point blow-up. In particular, using~\cite[Theorem~1.2]{Hu}, one can see that
\[
{\rm GW}^{\widetilde{M}}_{0,k+3,\widetilde{A}}\big(\widetilde{[pt]}_M,\widetilde{a}_0,\widetilde{[pt]}_M,\widetilde{a}_1\ldots,\widetilde{a}_k\big)={\rm GW}^M_{0,k+3,A}\big([pt]_M,a_0,[pt]_M,a_1\ldots,a_k\big)\neq 0,
\]
where $[pt]_M$ is the point class in $M$, $\widetilde{A}={\rm PD}_{\widetilde{M}}\widetilde{{\rm PD}_M(A)}$ and $\widetilde{a}_i={\rm PD}_{\widetilde{M}}\widetilde{{\rm PD}_M(a_i)}$ for all $i\in\{1,\ldots,k\}$. Since $\widetilde{[pt]}_M$ is indeed the point class $[pt]_{\widetilde{M}}$ on~$\widetilde{M}$, the nonzero Gromov-Witten invariant yields
\[
\Theta(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{\omega})\leq \langle[\widetilde{\omega}],\widetilde{A}\rangle=
\langle[\omega],A\rangle.
\]
This, together with Theorem~\ref{thm:mainthm}, leads to 
\begin{thm}\notag
Let $(M,\omega)$ be a closed symplectic manifold with  symplectic period $p>0$ which admits a nonzero Gromov-Witten invariant of the form~(\ref{e:blowup}). 
Assume that $(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{\omega})$ is the symplectic blow-up of $(M,\omega)$ of weight $\lambda$ with the property that $\pi\lambda^2=kp/m<c_G$ for some $k,m\in \N$. Then
\[
\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq \bigg\lceil\frac{p\cdot{\rm gcd}(m,k)\cdot\cl(\widetilde{M};\C)}{m\cdot\Theta(M,\omega)}\bigg\rceil
\]
for all $\varphi\in\ham(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{\omega})$.\end{thm}

\begin{rmk}
 In case that $\Theta(M,\omega)=p$, the above inequality reduces to
\[
\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq \bigg\lceil\frac{{\rm gcd}(m,k)\cdot\cl(\widetilde{M};\C)}{m}\bigg\rceil
\]
for all $\varphi\in\ham(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{\omega})$.  In particular, for $(\C P^n,\omega_{\rm FS})$, one can choose $k=1, m=2$, and hence gets
\[
\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq \big\lceil \cl(\widetilde{\C P^n};\C)/2\big\rceil=\lceil (n+1)/2 \rceil
\]
for any $\varphi\in\ham(\widetilde{\C P^n},\widetilde{\omega}_{\rm FS})$. We remark here that the blow-up $(\widetilde{\C P^n},\widetilde{\omega}_{\rm FS})$ in this case is not monotone except for $n=3$ since, otherwise, $\frac{\kappa (n+1)}{2}=\frac{p}{2}=\pi\lambda^2=\kappa(n-1)$ yields $n=3$.

Following McDuff-Polterovich~\cite{MP},  one can  blow up an almost K\"{a}hler manifold at finite many points, and this process of blowing up is closely related to the symplectic packing problem. Let $(M,\omega,J)$ be a symplectic manifold such that $\omega$ tames $J$. Assume that $J$ is integrable near the points $x_1,\ldots,x_k\in M$ and let $(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{J})$ be its complex blow-up at these points. Then there is a holomorphic map $\pi_M:(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{J})\to (M,J)$ which is a bijection over $M\setminus\{x_1,\ldots,x_k\}$ and maps the exceptional divisor to the points $x_j$, $j\in\{1,\ldots,k\}$. As before, we denote by $e_1,\ldots, e_k$ the cohomology classes on $\widetilde{M}$ which are Poincar\'{e} dual to the exceptional divisors. If
\[
\psi=\coprod_{j=1}^k\psi_j: \coprod_{j=1}^k(B(\lambda_j+\varepsilon),\omega,i)\longrightarrow (M,\omega,J)
\]
is a symplectic and holomorphic embedding of $k$ disjoint balls of radii $\lambda_j+\varepsilon$ into $M$ whose centers are mapped to the points $x_1,\ldots, x_k$, then by~\cite[Proposition~2.1B]{MP} one can construct a symplectic form $\widetilde{\omega}$ on $\widetilde{M}$ which is tamed by $\widetilde{J}$ and satisfies
\[
[\widetilde{\omega}]=[\pi_M^*\omega]-\sum_{j=1}^k\pi\lambda_j^2e_j. 
\]
Additionally, we assume that $(M,\omega)$ is a closed symplectic manifold with  minimal symplectic period $p>0$ which admits a nonzero Gromov-Witten invariant of the form~(\ref{e:blowup}). If one can choose $m,n_1,\ldots,n_k\in\N$ such that $\pi\lambda_j^2=pn_j/m$, then the minimal symplectic period of $(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{\omega})$ is $p\cdot {\rm gcd}(m,n_1,\ldots,n_k)/m$. Using~\cite[Theorem~1.2]{Hu} repeatedly and arguing as before, one can see that $(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{\omega})$ admits a nonzero Gromov-Witten invariant of the form~(\ref{e:blowup}) with 
$\langle[\widetilde{\omega}],\widetilde{A}\rangle\leq\Theta(M,\omega)$. Hence, by Theorem~\ref{thm:mainthm}, we obtain
\[
\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq \bigg\lceil\frac{p\cdot{\rm gcd}(m,n_1,\ldots,n_k)}{m\cdot\Theta(M,\omega)}\cdot\cl(\widetilde{M};\C)\bigg\rceil
\]
for all $\varphi\in\ham(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{\omega})$.


%If in addition $(M,\omega)$ and $(\widetilde{M},\widetilde{\omega})$ are monotone, then one can choose $k=n-1$ and $m=N$ (minimal Chern number of $(M,\omega)$) and hence obtain 

\end{rmk}




\subsubsection{Coadjoint orbits of $U(n)$}


We shall give symplectic fixed point estimates for the monotone coadjoint orbits of the unitary group $U(n)$ for which its Lie algebra is of type $A_{n-1}$ in the theory abstract root systems. For general monotone coadjoint orbits of compact simple Lie groups, one can obtain similar lower bound estimates for symplectic fixed points, see~Theorem~\ref{thm:orbits} in the appendix. Our approach is based on Caviedes Castro's  computation~\cite{CC} of the upper bound for the quantity $\Theta(M,\omega)$.


The Lie algebra of unitary group $U(n)$ is the space $\mathfrak{u}(n)$ of all $n\times n$ skew-Hermitian matrices. Take the maximal torus $T=U(1)^n$ of diagonal matrices in $U(n)$.
Its Lie algebra can be identified as
\[
\fT=\{(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\in\R^n\}.
\]
Let $\{e_1,\ldots,e_n\}$ be the standard basis of $\R^n$.  We choose the following adjoint invariant metric on $\mathfrak{u}(n)$ 
\[
\mathfrak{u}(n)\times\mathfrak{u}(n)\longrightarrow\R,\quad (X,Y)\longmapsto -{\rm Trace}(X,Y). 
\]
It turns out that the roots are of the form
\[
\alpha_{i,j}:\fT \longrightarrow\R,\quad (x_1,\ldots,x_n)\longmapsto x_i-x_j
\]
where $1\leq i\neq j\leq n$. Hence, under the identification $\fT\cong\fT^*$, we have $\alpha_{i,j}=e_i-e_j$, and the coroots are 
\[
\check{\alpha}_{i,j}=\frac{2(e_i-e_j)}{\langle e_i-e_j,e_i-e_j\rangle}=e_i-e_j. 
\]
The Weyl group is the symmetric group $S_n$ generated by the reflections
\[
s_{\alpha_{i,j}}:\R^n\longrightarrow\R^n,\quad
(x_1,\ldots,x_i,\ldots,x_j,\ldots,x_n)\mapsto (x_1,\ldots,x_j,\ldots,x_i,\ldots,x_n).
\]
The longest element $w_0$ in $S_n$ relative to the set of simple roots $\{\alpha_{i,i+1}|1\leq i\leq n-1\}$ is the permutation
\[
w_0:\R^n\longrightarrow\R^n,\quad (x_1\ldots,x_n)\to (x_n,\ldots,x_1).
\]

Now we consider the coadjoint orbit $\cO_\lambda$ of $\lambda=(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n)\in\R^n\setminus\{0\}$ with $\lambda_1\geq,\ldots,\geq\lambda_n$, i.e., 
\[
\cO_\lambda:=\big\{A\in\mathfrak{u}(n)|A^*=-A,\;\hbox{eigenvalues of}\;A\;\hbox{are}\;\sqrt{-1}\lambda_1,\ldots, \sqrt{-1}\lambda_n\big\}
\]
which is endowed with the Kostant-Kirillov-Souriau form $\omega_\lambda$. We choose $\lambda$ as above such that $(\omega_\lambda,\omega_\lambda)$ is monotone.  
Note that
\[
w_0=s_{\alpha_{1,n}} s_{\alpha_{2,n-1}}\cdots s_{\alpha_{[n/2],n-[n/2]+1}}.
\]
Combining this with~Theorem~\ref{thm:orbits},  one finds that
\[
\Theta(M,\omega)\leq \sum_{k=1}^{[n/2]}
\langle\lambda, \check{\alpha}_{k,n-k+1}\rangle=\sum_{k=1}^{[n/2]}\lambda_k-\lambda_{n-k+1}.
\]
Note also that the symplectic period of $(\cO_\lambda,\omega_\lambda)$ is
\[
p=\min_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\{\ell_{ij}\langle\lambda, \check{\alpha}_{i,j}\rangle>0|\ell_{ij}\in\Z\}=\min_{2\leq i\leq n}\{\ell_i(\lambda_{i-1}-\lambda_i)>0|\ell_i\in\Z\}.
\]
Consequently, by Theorem~\ref{thm:mainthm}, for any $\varphi\in\ham(\cO_\lambda,\omega_\lambda)$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{e:U}
\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq \bigg\lceil \frac{\min_{2\leq  i\leq n}\{\ell_i(\lambda_{i-1}-\lambda_i)>0|\ell_i\in\Z\}\cdot{\rm cuplength}(M;\C)}{\sum_{k=1}^{[n/2]}\lambda_k-\lambda_{n-k+1}} \bigg\rceil.
\end{equation}

\begin{rmk}
According to the theory of abstract root systems, there are only finite many types of root systems: type $A_{n-1}$, type $B_n$, type $C_n$, type $D_n$, types $E_6$, $E_7$, $E_8$, type $F_4$ and type $G_2$, see for instance~\cite{FH}. These types of abstract root systems  precisely correspond to the \textit{Dynkin diagrams}.  The symplectic fixed point estimate for remaining types can be done similarly, we omit them and refer the readers to~\cite[Section~9]{CC} and Appendix~\ref{sec:coadjoint}. 
\end{rmk}


\subsubsection{Monotone partial flag manifolds}
Given a sequence $0=n_0<n_1<,\ldots,<n_r<n_{r+1}=n$ of integers, the partial  flag manifold $F=F(n_1,\ldots,n_r,n)$ is a complex manifold consisting of all sequences
\[
0\subset V_1\subset\ldots\subset V_r\subset \C^n
\]
where each $V_i$ is a complex linear space with $\dim_\C(V_i)=n_i$. The complex dimension of $F(n_1,\ldots,n_r,n)$ is $\sum_{i=1}^r(n_i-n_{i-1})(n_{i+1}-n_i)$. Let $F_n$ denote the complete flag manifold $F(1,2,\ldots,n)$ for short. Let $\{e_1,\ldots,e_n\}$ be the standard basis of $\C^n$. 
Denote by $P=P(n_1,\ldots,n_r,n)\subset GL_n(\C)$  the  isotropic subgroup of the standard flag 
$$V_i={\rm span}\{e_1,\ldots,e_{n_i}\}.$$
Since the intersection of $P$ and $U(n)$ is $U(k_1)\times\ldots\times U(k_{r+1})$ with $k_i=n_i-n_{i-1}$ for $i=1,\ldots, r+1$, the partial flag manifold reads as
\[
F(n_1,\ldots,n_r,n)=GL_n(\C)/P=U(n)/U(k_1)\times\ldots\times U(k_{r+1}).
\]
In particular, the complete flag manifold can be written as 
$F_n=GL_n(\C)/B=U(n)/T$, where $B\subset GL_n(\C)$ is a Borel subgroup consisting of upper triangular invertible matrices, and $T$ is a maximal torus in $U(n)$ consisting of diagonal matrices.  

Next we give a coadjoint orbit description of flag manifolds. As before, we identify the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{u}(n)$ of the unitary group $U(n)$ with $\sqrt{-1}\mathfrak{u}(n)^*$ via a $U(n)$-invariant scalar product. Fix $\lambda={\rm diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n)\in-\sqrt{-1}\mathfrak{u}(n)$ with
\[
\underbrace{\lambda_1=\ldots=\lambda_{n_1}}_{k_1}>\underbrace{\lambda_{n_1+1}=\ldots=\lambda_{n_2}}_{k_2}>\ldots>\underbrace{\lambda_{n_r+1}=\ldots=\lambda_n}_{k_{r+1}}.
\]
Then one can identify the coadjoint orbit $\cO_\lambda\subset-\sqrt{-1}\mathfrak{u}(n)$ of $\lambda$ by 
\[
F=U(n)/U(k_1)\times\ldots\times U(k_{r+1})\longrightarrow \cO_\lambda,\quad [A]\longmapsto A\lambda A^*. 
\]
This gives $F$ a K\"{a}hler form $\omega$  via pulling back by the Kostant-Kirillov-Souriau form $\omega_\lambda$ on $\cO_\lambda$. It is shown in~\cite{NNU} that $c_1(T\cO_\lambda)=[\omega_\lambda]$ if and only if 
\[\lambda=(\underbrace{n-n_1,\ldots}_{k_1}, \underbrace{n-n_1-n_2,\ldots}_{k_2},\ldots,\underbrace{n-n_{r-1}-n_r,\ldots}_{k_r},\underbrace{-n_r,\ldots,-n_r}_{k_{r+1}})+(\underbrace{c,\ldots,c}_n)
\]
for some $c\in\R$. 

Now we give symplectic fixed point estimates for monotone  flag manifolds in the following cases:
\begin{itemize}
\item[1.] The complex projective space $\C P^{n-1}$ $\longleftrightarrow$ $\lambda=(n-1,\underbrace{-1,\ldots,-1}_{n-1})$.
\item[2.] The Grassmannian $Gr_\C(k,n)$ $\longleftrightarrow$ $\lambda=(\underbrace{n-k,\ldots,n-k}_k,\underbrace{-k,\ldots,-k}_{n-k})$.
\item[3.] The partial flag manifold $F(1,n-1,n)$ $\longleftrightarrow$ $\lambda=(n-1,\underbrace{0,\ldots,0}_{n-2},-n+1)$.
\item[4.] The partial flag manifold $U(n)/U(k)^m$ with $n=mk$ corresponds to $$\small\lambda=(\underbrace{(m-1)k,\ldots,(m-1)k}_k,\underbrace{(m-3)k,\ldots,(m-3)k}_k,\ldots,\underbrace{-(m-1)k\ldots,-(m-1)k}_k).$$
\item[5.] The complete flag manifold $F_n$ $\longleftrightarrow$ $\lambda=(\underbrace{n-1,n-3,\ldots, -n+1}_n)$.
\end{itemize}
Since ${\cl}(M;\C)={\dim}_\C(M)+1$ for any simply connected algebraic manifold $M$ (see~\cite{Be}),  we obtain $${\rm cuplength}(F(n_1,\ldots,n_r,n); \C)=\sum_{i=1}^r(n_i-n_{i-1})(n_{i+1}-n_i)+1.$$
Using (\ref{e:U}), we finds the following:\\
\noindent Case~1. For any $\varphi\in{\rm Ham}(\C P^{n-1},\omega)$ with $n\geq 2$, $\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq n$.\\
\noindent Case~2. For any $\varphi\in{\rm Ham}(Gr_\C(k,n),\omega)$, we have
$$\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq \bigg\lceil \frac{n\cdot(k(n-k)+1)}{n\cdot \min\{k,n-k\}} \bigg\rceil=\max\{k,n-k\}+1.$$
\noindent Case~3. For any $\varphi\in{\rm Ham}(F(1,n-1,n),\omega)$ with $n\geq 3$, 
$$\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq \bigg\lceil\frac{(n-1)\cdot 2(n-2 )}{2(n-1)} \bigg\rceil=n-2.$$

\noindent Case~4. For any $\varphi\in{\rm Ham}(U(n)/U(k)^m,\omega)$ with $n=mk$ and $m\geq 2$, 
$$\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq \bigg\lceil\frac{2k\cdot(k^2m(m-1)/2+1)}{2k(m-\lfloor m/2\rfloor)\cdot\lfloor m/2\rfloor} \bigg\rceil=2k^2-\bigg\lfloor\frac{k^2\lfloor m/2\rfloor-1}{\lfloor m/2\rfloor(m-\lfloor m/2\rfloor)} \bigg\rfloor.$$

\noindent Case~5. For any $\varphi\in{\rm Ham}(F_n,\omega)$, 
$$\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq \bigg\lceil\frac{2\cdot(n(n-1)/2+1)}{2(n-\lfloor n/2\rfloor)\cdot \lfloor n/2\rfloor} \bigg\rceil=2.$$

\begin{rmk}
Note that the complex Grassmanian $Gr_\C(k,n)$ with minimal Chern number $n$ has a $0$-nonnilpotent element of degree $\dim_\R (Gr_\C(k,n))-2$ given by the Poincar\'{e} dual of $c_1(TGr_\C(k,n))$ , see~\cite{Sc2}. By Corollary~\ref{cor:nonnilp}, we have $\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq n$ for all $\varphi\in{\rm Ham}(Gr_\C(k,n),\omega)$. This is a better lower bound estimate than that given in case 2. Similarly, 
for the complete flag manifold $F_n$ with minimal Chern number $2$, there is a $0$-nonnilpotent element of degree $\dim_\R (F_n)-2$, and hence we obtain the same estimate as in case~5, see also~\cite{Sc2}, which seems to be far from the expected result. 


\end{rmk}


\subsubsection{Fano toric manifolds}
Let $(M^{2m},\omega)$ be a toric  manifold,  namely a symplectic quotient $\C^n /\mkern-5mu/ \T^k$ of $\C^n$ by a subtorus $\T^k\subset\T^n$,  see~Definition~\ref{df:toric}. The homology class $H_{2m-2}(M;\Z)/{\rm torsion}$ is spanned by the classes $[X_1],\ldots,[X_n]$ which are given by $n$ smooth divisors $X_1,\ldots,X_n$ of $M$. For an $n$-tuple $d=(d_1,\dots,d_n)$ of nonnegative integers and a homology class $\alpha\in H_*(M;\Q)$, we abbreviate the following genus zero Gromov-Witten invariants of $M$ with fixed marked points in class $A\in H_2(M;\Z)$ 
\begin{equation}\label{e:GWA}
{\rm GW}_A([X]^{*d},\alpha):={\rm GW}^{\{1,\ldots,\sum_id_i+1\}}_{0,1+\sum_id_i,A}([X_1],\ldots,[X_1],\ldots,[X_n],\ldots,[X_n],\alpha)
\end{equation}
where each class $[X_i]$ occurs $d_i$ times. 
For each integer $\ell\geq 2$ and each  nonzero vector $d=(d_1,\ldots,d_n) $ with each integer $d_i\geq 0$ in $ \mathcal{D}^{\rm eff}(\tau)$ (this is a cone in $\Z^n$ corresponding to the set of effective classes in $H_2(M;\Z)/{\rm torsion}$, see~(\ref{e:cone})), according to Theorem~\ref{thm:fano}, if $M$ is Fano then we have  
\[
{\rm GW}_{A_{\ell \xi_d}}([X]^{*\ell d^+},[pt])={\rm GW}_{0,1,0}([pt])\neq 0,
\]
where the homology classes 
$A_{\ell \xi_d}\in H_2(M;\Z)/{\rm torsion}$ are  effective classes given by (\ref{e:class}) and (\ref{e:bi}), and the $n$-tuple $d^+=(d_1^+,\ldots,d_n^+)$ is given by
$d_i^+:=\max\{d_i,0\}$.  Since each divisor $X_i$ has codimension two,  
using Theorem~\ref{thm:onept} and letting $\ell\to\infty$ in the above nonzero Gromov-Witten invariants, we recover the following Givental's result. 
\begin{thm}[{\cite[Theorem~1.1]{Gi}}]
Let $(M,\omega)$ be a Fano toric  manifold with period $p>0$, $\omega(\pi_2(M))=p\cdot\Z$. Let $\mathcal{E}$ be the set of all nonzero effective homology classes of $M$. 
Then 
\[
\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq \sup_{A\in\mathcal{E}} \bigg\lceil \frac{p\cdot\langle c_1(TM),A\rangle}{\langle[\omega],A\rangle}\bigg\rceil.
\]
for any $\varphi\in\ham(M,\omega)$. 
In particular, if $(M,\omega)$ is a monotone toric manifold with minimal 
Chern number $N\geq 1$, then each Hamiltonian diffeomorphism $\varphi$ has at least $N$ fixed points. 
\end{thm}

\begin{rmk}
In~\cite[Theorem~1.1]{Gi}, Givental assumed that the symplectic form is primitive, i.e., $\omega(\pi_2(M))=\Z$. That's why the symplectic period $p$ does not occur in his lower bound. 
\end{rmk}

