\section{Coadjoint orbits of compact simple Lie groups}\label{sec:coadjoint} 
We shall employ Theorem~\ref{thm:mainthm} to obtain symplecitc fixed point estimates for monotone coadjoint orbits of compact simple Lie groups. Recall that a compact Lie group $G$ acts on its dual Lie algebra $\fG^*$ by the coadjoint representation. For $\lambda\in\fG^*$, the coadjoint orbit $\cO_\lambda$ passing through $\lambda$ is endowed with a canonical symplectic form $\omega_\lambda$, known as \textit{Kostant-Kirillov-Souriau form} (cf.~\cite{Ki}). Let $(\cdot,\cdot)$ denote an adjoint invariant inner product on $\fG$.
There is a canonical isomorphism 
$$\fG\longrightarrow\fG^*,\quad X\longmapsto (X,\cdot)$$
which induces a scalar product on $\fT^*$, denoted again by $(\cdot,\cdot)$.
Let $T\subset G$ be a maximal torus with Lie algebra $\fT\subset\fG$. Let $\mathfrak{R}\subset\fT^*$ be the root system of $T$. Hence, the complexifications of $\fG$ and $\fT$ satisfy the following decomposition
\[
\fG_\C=\fT_\C\oplus\bigoplus\limits_{\alpha\in\mathfrak{R}}\fG_\alpha
\]
where $\fG_\alpha$ is the root space for which the \textit{root} $\alpha\in \mathfrak{R}$ is given by
\[
\fG_\alpha:=\big\{Y\in\fG_\C\;|\;[X,Y]=\sqrt{-1}\alpha(X)Y,\quad \hbox{for all}\;X\in\fT\big\}.
\]
Corresponding to each root $\alpha\in\mathfrak{R}$, one has a \textit{coroot} $\check{\alpha}\in\fT$ identified with $2\alpha/(\alpha,\alpha)\in\fT^*$ via the above invariant inner product. Given $\alpha\in\mathfrak{R}$, we define a reflection $s_\alpha$ on $\fT^*$ as
\[
s_\alpha: \fT^*\longrightarrow \fT^*,\quad \xi\longmapsto \xi-\frac{2(\alpha,\xi)}{(\alpha,\alpha)}\alpha.\]
Fix a subset $\mathcal{S}\subset \mathfrak{R}$ consisting of simple roots $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_\ell$ such that 
any $\alpha\in  \mathfrak{R}$  can be written as 
\[
\alpha=\sum_{k=1}^\ell m_k\alpha_k
\]
where $m_k\in\Z$ are all in $\Z_{\geq 0}$ or all in $\Z_{\leq 0}$. The roots $\alpha$ for which all $m_k$ are in $\Z_{\geq 0}$ (reps. $\Z_{\leq 0}$) are called positive (resp. negative). 
Write $\mathfrak{R}=\mathfrak{R}^+\cup \mathfrak{R}^-$ according to the positive and negative roots. Obviously, we have $\mathcal{S}\subset \mathfrak{R}^+$. 

Let $G_\C$ be the complexification of $G$. We choose a parabolic subgroup of $P\subset G_\C$ such that $\cO_\lambda\cong G_\C/P$. This allows us to endow $\cO_\lambda$ with a complex structure $J$ compatible with $\omega_\lambda$ so that the triple $(\cO_\lambda,\omega_\lambda,J)$ is a K\"{a}hler manifold.  
Fix a Borel subgroup $B\subset P$ which has Lie algebra
\[
\mathfrak{b}=\fT_\C\oplus\bigoplus_{\alpha\in\mathfrak{R}^+}\fG_\alpha. 
\]
The group $W$ of linear transformations of $
\fT^*$ generated by all reflections $s_\alpha$, $\alpha\in\mathfrak{R}$ is called the \textit{Weyl group} of $G$ relative to $T$. It is in fact generated by $s_{\alpha_1},\ldots,s_{\alpha_\ell}$, and is isomorphic to the quotient group $N_{
G_\C}(T)/T$ where $N_{G_\C}(T)$ is the normalizer of $T$ in $G_\C$. The \textit{length} $L(w)$ of an element $w$ of $W$ is the minimum number of simple reflections whose
product is $w$. The element of longest length is denoted by $w_0$. The parabolic subgroup $P$ of $G_\C$ corresponds canonically to a subset $\mathcal{S}_P =\{\alpha\in\mathcal{S}|\langle\lambda,\check{\alpha}\rangle= 0\}$. Let $\mathfrak{R}^+_P$ be the set of positive roots can be written as sums of roots in $\mathcal{S}_P$. We denote by $W_P$ the Weyl group of $P$ generated by the set of simple roots in $\mathcal{S}_P$. 

For $u\in W/W_P$, we let $X(u)=\overline{BuP/P}$ be the corresponding \textit{Schubert variety}. Here the $u$ should first be interpreted as a representative in $W$ and then a representative in the normalizer $N_{G_\C}(T)$ due to the fact that $X(u)$ is independent of these choices. This is a subvariety of $G_\C/P$ of real dimension $2L(u)$. Let $B^-:=w_0Bw_0\subset G_\C$ be the \textit{Borel subgroup opposite} to $B$.  Similarly, we let $Y(u)=\overline{B^-uP/P}$ be the \textit{opposite Schubert variety} of codimension $2L(u)$. Denote by $\sigma_u$ and $\check{\sigma}_u$ the fundamental classes in the homology group $H_*(G_\C/P;\Z)$ of $Y(u)$ and $X(u)$, respectively. It is well known that the set of Schubert classes $\{\sigma_u\}_{u\in W/W_P}$ forms a $\Z$-basis of $H_*(G_\C/P;\Z)$, and the set of Schubert classes $\{\check{\sigma}_u\}_{u\in W/W_P}$ is the dual basis of $\{\sigma_u\}_{u\in W/W_P}$ with respect to the Poincar\'{e} intersection pairing. 

For any $u$ in $W/W_P$, we let $x(u) = uP/P$ be the corresponding point in $G_\C/P$. Such points are all $T$-fixed points of  $G_\C/P$. The varieties $X(u)$ and $Y (u)$ meet transversally at the point $x(u)$. Corresponding to a positive root $\alpha\in\mathfrak{R}\setminus\mathfrak{R}_P^+$, we have a unique irreducible $T$-invariant curve $C_\alpha=SL_2(\C)_\alpha\cdot P/P$ containing $1\cdot P$ and $s_\alpha\cdot P$, where $SL_2(\C)_\alpha\subset G_\C$ is the subgroup with Lie algebra $\fG_\alpha\oplus\fG_{-\alpha}\oplus [\fG_\alpha,\fG_{-\alpha}]$. Such $C_\alpha$ has the symplectic area $\langle\lambda, \check{\alpha}\rangle$ with respect to the symplectic form $\omega_\lambda$. If $\alpha\in \mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{S}_P$, then $C_\alpha=X(s_\alpha)$ is one of the basic Schubert varieties, see~\cite{FH,FWo}. Hence, there is a one-by-one map
\[
\Z\check{\mathcal{S}}/\Z\check{\mathcal{S}}_P\longrightarrow H_2(G_\C/P;\Z),\quad \check{\alpha}+\Z  \check{\mathcal{S}}_P\longmapsto [C_\alpha]
\]
where $\Z\check{\mathcal{S}}:=\{k\check{\alpha}|\alpha\in\mathcal{S},\;k\in\Z\}$ and $\Z\check{\mathcal{S}}_P:=\{k\check{\alpha}|\alpha\in\mathcal{S}_P,\;k\in\Z\}$.

With the above preparation we now cite a result of Caviedes Castro~\cite{CC} on the estimate of  the quantity $\Theta(\cO_\lambda,\omega_\lambda)$ which is used to bound Hofer-Zehnder capacity of coadjoint orbits of compact Lie groups via Lu's theorem~\cite[Corollary~1.19]{Lu1}.

\begin{lem}[{\cite[Theorem~5.2]{CC}}]
Let $G$ be a compact simple Lie group, and $(\cO_\lambda,\omega_\lambda)$ a monotone coadjoint orbit passing through $\lambda\in\fT^*$. If there exist orthogonal positive roots $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_r\in\mathfrak{R}$ such that the longest element $w_0$ of the Weyl group $W$ has the composition $w_0=s_{\alpha_1}\cdots s_{\alpha_r}$, then 
\[
\Theta(\cO_\lambda,\omega_\lambda)\leq \sum_{k=1}^r|\langle \lambda,\check{\alpha}_k\rangle|.
\]
\end{lem}
Combining the above lemma with Theorem~\ref{thm:mainthm}, we obtain

\begin{thm}\label{thm:orbits}
Let $G$ be a compact simple Lie group, and $(\cO_\lambda,\omega_\lambda)$ a monotone coadjoint orbit passing through $\lambda\in\fT^*$. Then
\begin{equation}\label{e:adjorbit}
\#{\rm Fix}(\varphi)\geq \sup\limits_{\substack{\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_r\in\mathfrak{R},\\w_0=s_{\alpha_1}\cdots s_{\alpha_r}}}\bigg\lceil \frac{p\cdot{\rm cuplength}(M;\C)}{\sum_{k=1}^r|\langle \lambda,\check{\alpha}_k\rangle|} \bigg\rceil
\end{equation}
for all $\varphi\in\ham(\cO_\lambda,\omega_\lambda)$,  where $$p=\min\bigg\{\sum_{i=1}^\ell\ell_i\langle \lambda,\check{\alpha}_i\rangle>0\;\big|\;\alpha_i\in\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{S}_P,\; \ell_i\in\Z\bigg\}$$ is the minimal sympletic period of  $(\cO_\lambda,\omega_\lambda)$. 
\end{thm}





\begin{rmk}
In~\cite{CC}  Caviedes Castro used the monotonicity condition to identify the algebraic Gromov–Witten invariant defined on complex homogeneous projective manifolds with the symplectic Gromov–Witten invariants, and then resorted to the works of Fulton-Woodward~\cite{FWo} and Postnikov~\cite{Po} to obtain the upper bound of $\Theta(\cO_\lambda,\omega_\lambda)$. If the equivalence of these two definitions of symplectic Gromov–Witten invariants and their algebraic counterparts holds true in general, then one can drop the monotonicity assumption and still has the inequality~(\ref{e:adjorbit}). %However, it has not been fully established in the literature for complex projective manifolds.

\end{rmk}


\section{Symplectic toric manifolds}\label{Sec:toric}
\setcounter{thm}{0} 
\renewcommand{\thethm}{B.\arabic{thm}}



We recall here a definition and some facts on symplectic toric manifolds, and refer the reader to the books~\cite{Au,MS} for more details on this subject. 

Let $\C^n$ be the standard $2n$-dimensional real vector space equipped with the standard symplectic form $\omega_0$, i.e., the imaginary part of $-\frac{1}{2}\sum dz_i\wedge d\bar{z}_i$. Let $\T^k$ be a $k$-dimensional torus with Lie algebra $\mathfrak{t}$. Denote the integer lattice of $\mathfrak{t}$ by 
\[
\Delta:=\{\xi\in\fT\;|\;\exp (\xi)=1\}
\]
and its dual lattice by 
\[
\Delta^*:=\{w\in \mathfrak{t}^*|\langle w,\xi\rangle\in\Z\;\;\hbox{for any}\;\xi\in\Delta\}. 
\]
Suppose that $\T^k$ acts on $\C^n$ by Hamiltonian linear transformations
\[
z_i\longmapsto \exp(-2\pi\sqrt{-1}\langle \fw_i,\xi \rangle)z_i,\quad i=1,\ldots, n
\]
where $\fw_i\in \Delta^*$. The moment map  of this action is given by 
\[
\mu(z)=\pi\sum_{i=1}^n|z_i|\fw_i 
\]
for any $z\in\C^n$. Here we require that $\mu$ is proper and the vectors $\fw_i$ span the vector space $\fT^*$, and hence the linear space of solutions $(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_n)\in\R^n$ for the linear equation $\sum_{i=1}^n\alpha_i\fw_i=0$ in $\fT^*$ has dimension $n-k$. 

\begin{df}\label{df:toric}
A toric symplectic manifold is a symplectic quotient
at a regular value $\tau\in \fT^*$ of the moment map $\mu$ by $\T^k$ such that $\T^k$ acts freely on $\mu^{-1}(\tau)$, namely
\[
M_\tau=\mu^{-1}(\tau)/\T^k
\]
which has a real dimension $2m=2n-2k$ and a symplectic form $\omega_\tau\in \Omega^2(M_\tau)$ induced by the symplectic reduction of $(\C^n,\omega_0)$.
\end{df}
We denote by $H^*(M_\tau)$, respectively  $H_*(M_\tau)$, the quotients of the integral (co)homology groups by their torsion subgroups. 
For $j\in I:=\{1,\ldots,n\}$, we denote by 
\[
{\rm cone}(I_j)=\big\{\sum_{i\neq j}\alpha_i\fw_i\;\big|\;\alpha_i\geq 0\big\}
\]
the positive cone spanned by the vectors $\fw_i,\; i\in I\setminus\{j\}$. Consider now the subspace
$$E_j=\{z=(z_1,\ldots,z_n)\in\C^n\;|\;z_j=0\}.$$
Obviously, $\tau\in {\rm cone}(I_j)$ if and only if $E_j\cap\mu^{-1}(\tau)\neq 0$. In this case, one can see that the corresponding submanifold 
\[
X_j=E_j\cap\mu^{-1}(\tau)/\T^k=\{[z]\in M_\tau\;|\;z_j=0\}. 
\]
is a smooth divisor of $M_\tau$ and determines a codimension-$2$ class $[X_j]$ in $H_{2m-2}(M_\tau)$.  It is shown in \cite{GuS} that $[X_j]$ ($1\leq j\leq n$) generate the homology ring of $H_*(M_\tau)$ w.r.t. the usual homology intersection product $\cap$ in the following sense: 
\begin{itemize}
\item the homology classes $[X_1],\ldots, [X_n]$ span $H_{2m-2}(M_\tau)$,
\item every relation $\sum_{i=1}^n\alpha_i\fw_i=0$ implies a corresponding relation $\sum_{i=1}^n\alpha_i[X_i]=0$,
\item for any subset $J=\{i_1,\ldots, i_j\}\subset I$, the relation $\tau\notin \{\sum_{i\in I\setminus J}\alpha_i\fw_i\;\big|\;\alpha_i\geq 0\}$ implies a corresponding relation $[X_{i_1}]\cap\cdots\cap[X_{i_j}]=0$,
\item the above relations are the only ones among the classes $[X_i]$ in $H_*(M_\tau)$. 
\end{itemize}
From this fact one can see that $H_2(M_\tau)$ can be identified with the sublattice of $\Delta\subset\fT$:
\[\Delta(\tau)=\{\xi\in\Delta\;|\;\langle \fw_j,\xi\rangle=0\quad \hbox{for any}\;j\;\hbox{with}\;\tau\notin {\rm cone}(I_j) \},\]
and we denote the bijective map by
\begin{equation}\label{e:class}
\Delta(\tau)\longrightarrow H_2(M_\tau),\quad \eta\longmapsto A_\eta.
\end{equation}
Then, the dual $H^2(M_\tau)$ corresponds to a quotient of the integer lattice in $\fT^*$. Further,  the first Chern class $c_1(TM)$ and the cohomology class of $\omega_\tau$ are given by 
\[c_1(TM)=\overline{\fw}_1+\ldots+\overline{\fw}_n,\quad [\omega_\tau]=\overline{\tau}\]
where $\overline{\gamma}$ is the image of $\gamma\in\fT^*$ under the above correspondence
\[
\fT^*=\Delta^*\otimes\R\longrightarrow H^2(M_\tau;\R), \quad \gamma\longmapsto \overline{\gamma}.
\]
Let $C(\tau)\subset\fT^*$ be the component of $\tau$ in the set of regular values of the moment map $\mu$ which is called the \textit{K\"{a}hler cone} of $M_\tau$. Its dual cone in $\Delta(\tau)$ is given by 
\[
\Delta^{\rm eff}(\tau)=\{\xi\in \Delta(\tau)\;|\;\langle t,\xi\rangle\geq 0\quad\hbox{for any}\;t\in C(\tau)\}.
\]
It is well known that each nontrivial class in $H_2(M_\tau)$ corresponding to an element in $\Delta^{\rm eff}(\tau)$ can be represented by a holomorphic curve in $M_\tau$. Such classes are called \textit{effective classes}. 
\begin{df}
We say that a toric manifold $M_\tau$ is \textit{Fano} if the vector $c_1=\sum_{i=1}^n\fw_i$ satisfies $\langle c_1,\xi\rangle>0$ for all $\xi\in \Delta^{\rm eff}(\tau)\setminus\{0\}$.  
\end{df}
Let $\mathcal{D}^{\rm eff}(\tau)\subset\Z^n$ denote the cone
\begin{equation}\label{e:cone}
\mathcal{D}^{\rm eff}(\tau)=\{(\langle\fw_1,\xi\rangle,\ldots,\langle\fw_n,\xi\rangle)\;|\;\xi\in \Delta^{\rm eff}(\tau)\}. 
\end{equation} 
Then we have a bijection $\Delta^{\rm eff}(\tau)\to \mathcal{D}^{\rm eff}(\tau)$, and denote its inverse map by
\begin{equation}\label{e:bi}
\mathcal{D}^{\rm eff}(\tau)\longrightarrow\Delta^{\rm eff}(\tau),\quad d\longmapsto \xi_d.
\end{equation} 
For $\eta\in\Delta(\tau)$, we denote by $A_\eta\in H_2(M_\tau) $ the corresponding homology class as in~(\ref{e:class}). Let 
${\rm GW}_A([X]^{*d},\alpha)$ denote the Gromov-Witten invariants as in  (\ref{e:GWA}). 
From the Batyrev-Givental formula for the quantum homology of $M_\tau$, it is not hard to  see the following
\begin{thm}{\cite[Theorem~11.3.3]{MS}}\label{thm:fano}
If  $M_\tau$ is a Fano toric manifold, then the equality
\[
{\rm GW}_{A_\eta}([X]^{*d^+},\alpha)={\rm GW}_{A_\eta-A_{\xi_d}}([X]^{*d^-},\alpha)
\]
holds for each $d\in\mathcal{D}^{\rm eff}(\tau)$, each $\eta\in\Delta(\tau)$ and each $\alpha\in H_*(M_\tau)$ with the $n$-tuples $d^+$ and $d^-$ given by
$$d_i^+:=\max\{d_i,0\},\quad d_i^-:=\max\{-d_i,0\}.$$
\end{thm}



