\abstract{
The apparent invariance of the strong nuclear force under combined charge conjugation and parity (\textit{CP}) remains an open question in modern physics. Precision experiments with heavy atoms and molecules can provide stringent constraints on \textit{CP} violation via searches for effects due to permanent electric dipole moments and other \textit{CP}-odd properties in leptons, hadrons, and nuclei. Radioactive molecules have been proposed as highly sensitive probes for such searches, but experiments with most such molecules have so far been beyond technical reach. Here we report the first production and spectroscopic study of a gas-phase actinium molecule, $^{227}$AcF. We observe the predicted strongest electronic transition from the ground state, which is necessary for efficient readout in searches of symmetry-violating interactions. 
% Electronic- and nuclear-structure calculations are performed and used in a global analysis that incorporates existing atomic and molecular searches of nuclear, hadronic, and leptonic \textit{CP}-odd properties. It is found that an experiment of realistic technological readiness using $^{227}$AcF that reaches a precision of 1~mHz would improve the current constraints on the \textit{CP} violation parameter hyperspace by three orders of magnitude. Our results thus highlight the potential of $^{227}$AcF for exceptionally sensitive searches of \textit{CP} violation using precision experiments.
Furthermore, we perform electronic- and nuclear-structure calculations for $^{227}$AcF to determine its sensitivity to various \textit{CP}-violating parameters, and find that a realistic, near-term experiment with a precision of 1\,mHz would improve current constraints on the \textit{CP}-violating parameter hyperspace by three orders of magnitude. Our results thus highlight the potential of $^{227}$AcF for exceptionally sensitive searches of \textit{CP} violation.
}
\keywords{CP violation, laser spectroscopy, radioactive molecules, quantum chemistry, nuclear theory}


