%---------------------------------
\subsubsection{Critical exceptional Point}
%---------------------------------
%---------------------------------
Based on the previous analysis, we can identify a Takens-Bogdanov point \cite{bogdanov1975versal,Takens1974b}, a degeneracy where both the real and imaginary parts of the eigenvalues $\lambda_{\pm}$ simultaneously vanish. At this point, nowadays sometimes called a ``critical exceptional point'' \cite{PhysRevX.14.021014, fruchart2021non, doi:10.1126/science.aar7709} the linear stability matrix $\underline{\mathbf{L}}$ becomes nondiagonalizable due to the coalescence of both eigenvalues and eigenvectors. Such points are associated with qualitative changes in the bifurcation behavior at the related Takens-Bogdanov bifurcation \cite{DaKn1987ptrslsapes,RuKn2016ds}. In our context, such a point can only occur for $\tilde{J}_{a} \neq \tilde{J}_{b}$, at the parameter value
\begin{equation*}
    \tilde{K}_{a}\tilde{K}_{b} = \mathcal{K}_{2}(\tilde{J}_{a},\tilde{J}_{b}),
\end{equation*}
and at the specific wavenumber
\begin{equation*}
    |\mathbf{k}| = k_{\rm R}=k^{\pm}_{\rm I},
\end{equation*}
as shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig2}(c),(f). At this point, the dispersion relation undergoes a transition from regimes dominated by either purely stationary or oscillatory unstable modes to a singular case in which neither dominates and the system exhibits nontrivial mode coalescence.
