\section{Discussions}

Rosettes are polarized, transient epithelial structures, observed as intermediate cellular structures in several biological contexts as different as morphogenesis, development, wound healing and cancer~\cite{Harding2014, Moran2023}.
%Modeling the cellular Vertex models describe the mechanical properties and dynamics of tessellations in materials science and biology. Energy is typically associated with vertices through contributions from line tensions (edges) and cell pressures (areas). A special case involves Voronoi tessellations, which approximate many biological and physical systems.
From a geometrical point of view, these structure can be identified in tesselation of 2D section of cellular layers as vertex where five or more edges meet. The mechanism through which such a configuration can be reached is often proposed to be a topological rearrangement following the disappearing of a cell, i.e. a T2 transition, either due to ablation or cell apoptosis~\cite{Harding2014}. Indeed, theoretical and numerical investigations adopted this framework to generate rosettes and study their effects on the tissue dynamics. Using modified vertex models, it has been found that rosettes impact on tissue fluidity and organization (see \textit{e.g.} the work of Yan and Bi \textit{et al.}~\cite{Yan_2019}). 
However, rosette formation is not necessarily due to cell ablations. For instance, intercalations give rise to multiple T1 transitions leading to a 5 or more-fold vertex ~\cite{Harding2014, Fletcher2014}, while preserving the number of cells in the population.

Thus, we sought for a more general driving principle  behind the formation of rosettes. 
We started by noting that in a flat Euclidean space, the energetically optimal tiling for cell systems ( but also  atomic crystals or soap films) is typically hexagonal. In such flat spaces, 3-fold vertices (T-junctions) are  energetically optimal because at a 3-edge vertex, one can arrange the edges at $120^\circ$ angles (like for hexagons), balancing the tensions symmetrically. This configuration preserves both local and global spatial symmetry. 
Four edges junctions with angles around $90^\circ$ are possible, but result in unstable configurations without special conditions (i.e. peculiar disposition of the line tensions).
Indeed,  generalizing Plateau's laws for soap films,  at equilibrium the angles at a vertex depend on (i) the number of edges meeting, (ii) the tensions in each edge, and (iii) the local curvature of the space.
Thus, higher-fold junctions could in principle become favorable for specific anisotropic tension configurations and/or where curvature affects the local angular sum at vertices.   
Positive curved space corresponds to sum of angles exceeding $360^\circ$. As the natural meeting angles between edges change, in this scenario rosettes can become stable because distributing larger angles over more edges reduces mechanical stress.

To test our intuition, we considered a population of cells described by an Active Voronoi model subject to the standard Vertex energy in Eq.~\ref{eq:vertex-ene}, moving in a box with periodic boundary conditions, i.e. on a toroid. Such energy is known to produce a regular hexagonal tiling for a value of the target shape factor of $s_0 \simeq 3.72$ and a solid to liquid-like transition for $s_0 \simeq 3.82$. In the solid region, the system exhibits cells with both pentagonal and heptagonal shapes (see polygon distributions in Figure~\ref{fig:1}). This behavior can be understood by means of the Euler's rule for a tessellation: $V - E + F = \chi$, 
where $V$ is the number of vertices, $E$ the number of edges, $F$ the number of faces, and $\chi$ is the Euler characteristic~\cite{DelBono2024} (note that for a flat plane, $\chi = 0$, while for a sphere it is 2).
If each vertex connects three edges, it follows that the average number of sides per face must be 6, leading to a preference for hexagonal patterns. Note that the presence of more than three edge vertices reduces the average. 

Values of the shape factor different from $p_0^{hex}$ introduces frustrations in the system, which are compensated by the introduction of defects such as pentagons and heptagons. Each cell's deviation from hexagonal geometry inserts a topological charge, %For instance, pentagons  produces a $+1$ charge, while  heptagon a $-1$ charge. 
, whose sum in flat space ($\chi=0$) must vanish. 
Such frustration is not sufficient to account for the presence of rosettes (see Figure~\ref{fig:1}). 

Indeed, in flat surfaces, high-order vertices tend to resolve back into 3-fold vertices.  In presence of a potential, rosettes can persist and balance mechanical forces and curvature constraints.
Moreover, positive curvature induces cells to tighten, which reflects in cells with low number of sides (see polygon distributions in Figure~\ref{fig:1} and ~\ref{fig:2}).

Introducing the external harmonic field via  Eq.~\ref{eq:tot_force_harm}, we inserted the simplest positive correction the curvature. 
To accommodate such curvature, the system tends to introduce topological defects that locally reduce angular excess, forming rosette like structures (see snapshots in Figure~\ref{fig:2}). Euler's characteristic becomes $\chi > 0$,  modifying the balance of defects:   an excess of positive topological charge is required.

%In curved spaces, Euler's rule modifies the balance of defects:  on a sphere ($\chi=2$), there must be an excess of positive topological charge (e.g., pentagons), while on a hyperbolic surface ($\chi<0$), an excess of negative charges (e.g., heptagons) is necessary.

Note that from a topological point of view, the necessity of these defects in curved geometries is explained by the Gauss-Bonnet theorem, which links the integrated Gaussian curvature of a surface to the total topological defect charge. For instance, to effectively tile a sphere, one has to introduce 5-fold disclinations
%, each contributing a 60 degree angular surplus, summing to the 720 degrees required to match the sphere's total curvature.
as is the case for  physical structures like fullerenes, viral capsids, and colloidal crystals on droplets~\cite{Drozdowski2024}.

From a physical perspective, defects carry an elastic or energetic cost and act like quasi-particles with topological charge. They interact with each other and with the underlying curvature, minimizing the total energy. This dynamic plays out in soft matter systems, liquid crystals, and 2D materials like graphene~\cite{Wen2013}. 

In ideal, infinite crystalline systems with flat geometry, translational symmetry ensures that the structure can be shifted in space without altering its physical properties. However, the introduction of curvature and the accompanying defects break this symmetry. On curved surfaces,  there is no global translational symmetry—each point has different geometric properties. Defects such as 5- and 7-fold polygons are therefore indicators of the breaking of translational invariance. In fact, this symmetry breaking is a hallmark of systems constrained by global geometry and is essential to the emergence of localized, inhomogeneous physical properties.

In the present work, we produced the breaking of the space translational invariance that generates topological defects via the introduction of either a potential energy landscape or a leader cell with different size with respect to the rest of the population.
In the former case,  the external potentials used can mimic the collective effects of apical actin filament networks. Indeed, actin filaments play a fundamental role in shaping the mechanical environment of cells, particularly by forming networks that exert tensile forces across the apical surface of epithelial cell layers. These supra-cellular actin networks can generate coordinated mechanical tension, contributing to tissue morphogenesis and the maintenance of epithelial integrity~\cite{FernandezGonzalez2011}.  In particular, it has been  demonstrated how apical actomyosin cables and medial actin structures contribute to mechanical force distribution across cell layers~\cite{Rauzi2010, FernandezGonzalez2011}.
Similarly, the potential can simulate the effect of an actual curved surface where cells are constrained to move like in embryos and tumor spheroids~\cite{Trichas2012}. 
Note that in these real systems, there is a spontaneous  breaking of the symmetry as internal cell mechanisms are responsible for processes like the accumulation of actin fibers or the shape of the embryo. 
Indeed, we show that the presence of a large cell in the layer suffices to insert a topological defect that  provoke the formation of transient rosettes.  

Relying on a dynamical model, we were able to probe the stabilizing/destabilizing effects of cellular properties on the formation of rosettes. In particular, we found that cell motility and deformability influence the formation and maturation of rosettes. 
Indeed, our simulations highlighted an interplay between topological curvature, self propulsion velocity and target shape factor (Figure~\ref{fig:2}b-c). In presence of fast moving cells, rosettes formation needs both an increase of space curvature  and a higher cell deformability. Similarly,  both the size and polarization of lumen centered structures is favored in presence of a cell population in a fluid-like state.
Notably, analyzing the behavior of populations of  
iPSC-derived neural cells growing on a 2D layer via  extensive fluorescent microscopy time lapses, we confirmed our model predictions, as rosettes form in regions characterized by slow moving cells having shape factor higher than 3.9, i.e. being in a fluid like regime. 
Looking the motion of isolated cells, we observed that their motion is maximum when it is aligned with the direction for their elongation (Figure~\ref{fig:6}).  
Self-alignment interactions that produce cell displacements in the same direction along which cells are elongated is the second ingredient we identified 
that  increases the probability of transient rosette formation (see Figure~\ref{fig:3}) and the size of lumen-centered ones (see Figure~\ref{fig:4}). In particular, it allows cells to orderly dispose in multiple layers around the lumen as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:5}.


In conclusion, our work proved that rosettes are natural outcomes of geometric and mechanical responses to curvature, providing an additional framework where the interplay between defects and spatial curvature offers profound insights into how materials and surfaces %(even spacetime itself) 
are structured, with deep implications across disciplines as diverse as condensed matter physics and cosmology. To test our prediction, we measured the dynamical features of populations of iPSCs and found that neural rosettes are observed when cells populate the region of the parameter space our model predicts compatible for their formation.

