\section{Materials and Methods}
\label{sec:MatMet}

\subsection{Cell Culture}
Human iPSCs from the WT I line~\cite{Lenzi_2015} were stably transfected with a construct encoding an RFP-tagged version of the nuclear FUS protein~\cite{De_Santis_2019}. iPSCs were induced to differentiate along the neuroectodermal lineage to form neural rosettes as follows. 10.000 cells/cm2 were plated onto glass bottom dishes (WillCo) coated with Matrigel ( in Nutristem XF/FF medium (Biological Industries) supplemented for 24 hours with 10 µM ROCK inhibitor (Y-27632, Sigma-Aldrich). After 2 days, medium was replaced with KSR medium (DMEM/F12 ; 15\% knockout serum replacement; 1X Glutamax; 1X non-essential amino acids; 1X penicillin-streptomycin; all from Life Technologies). This is considered day 0 of differentiation. From day 4 to day 10, KSR medium was gradually shifted to N2M medium (DMEM/F12; 1X N2 supplement; 1X Glutamax; 1X non-essential amino acids; 1X penicillin-streptomycin; all from Life Technologies). 10 µM SB431542 (Miltenyi Biotec) and 500 nM LDN193189 (Sigma-Aldrich) were added from day 0 to day 7. From day 10 onwards the medium was supplemented with 20 ng/ml BDNF, 20 ng/ml GDNF (PeproTech) and 0.2 mM ascorbic acid were added from day 10 onwards.

\subsection{Immunofluorescence staining}
After 11 days from the beginning of the iPSCs differentiation, cells were fixed in PFA4\% and permeabilized in Triton X-100 0.2\% for 5'. After a blocking stage in BSA 1\% (1h at RT), cells are incubated overnight with the anti-N-cadherin antibody (Ab18203; 5ug/ml) followed by a second stage of hybridization with a Goat anti-Rabbit Secondary Antibody, Alexa Fluor-647 (A-21244). 


\subsection{Time-lapse and time-course Microscopy}
\subsubsection{Microscopy Setup.} We used two microscopy setups: a spinning disk microscope and a Custom Timelapse Epifluorescence Microscope. Rosettes emerge sparsely distributed at random locations on the culture plate. 
For this reason, we selected two microscopy setup that allowed us to follow large areas of the culture plate. Both microscope were equipped with  stage top incubator. The spinning disk setup had a motorized stage and allowed the acquisition of multiple ROIs and large mosaic images. The custom microscopy was optimized for long 11-days time lapse acquisitions.
\textbf{Spinning Disk.} This setup is an Olympus microscope with a 20x objective equipped with a stage-top CO2 incubator designed for acquiring time-lapse sequences over a period of 1-2 weeks and a motorized stage. This setup allowed us to perform time-course experiments on two culture plates simultaneously, and time-lapse experiments on a single plate.
\textbf{Custom Timelapse Epifluorescence Microscope.} This setup is composed of an in-house assembled costume epifluorescence microscope with a 10x objective and a stage-top CO2 incubator designed for acquiring time-lapse sequences over 1-2 weeks. This set-up results in a 2 mm field width. 
\subsubsection{Timecourse}
The time-course experiments were performed on the spinning disk microscope. The time-course experiment allowed us to acquire large mosaics from 2 different culture plates, 3 times a day. Monitoring a large area of the culture plate, we captured the emergence of more than one rosette at a time. 
%We used the RFP channel to capture the fluorescente nuclei. These time courses were used to measure how the structure factor dynamically changes in correspondence and not of where rosettes emerge [pixel sizes: 0.225 micron/pixel] 
\subsubsection{Timelapse}
We had two different time-lapse protocols, one for the custom microscope and one for the spinning disk microscope.
The time-lapse experiment used for the evaluation of the dynamical cell features   
