\subsection{Single cell tracking}

Cell nuclei were tracked using a frame-to-frame approach. First, we used the nuclear fluorescence obtained with the reporter RFP protein expressed on the FUS protein to detect single cell nuclei. Second, the nuclei detected in each frame were
linked to the nuclei in the successive frames with links using a nuclei-nuclei distance cost function. Frame-to-frame tracking was implemented using the linear assignment problem (LAP) method [22,23]. 


\subsection{Diffusion Analysis}
From these trajectories, we obtained the different time intervals ($\Delta t$) and displacements ($\Delta x$) pairs, which we used to estimate the persistence time and length.
Trajectories obtained with the tracking pipeline described above were used to extract pairs of time intervals ($\Delta t$) and displacements ($\Delta x$), enabling estimation of dynamical quantities such as the Brownian exponent $\alpha$, the motility coefficient $v_0$ and the correlation time $\tau$. Coordinates were first converted from pixels to microns ($1,\mathrm{pixel}=0.650,\mu\mathrm{m}$).

Firstly, considering the behaviour of each cell’s positions $(x_t, y_y)$ versus the time, data were plotted on a log-log scale by displaying cell displacements $\Delta r$ against time intervals $\Delta t$, revealing two distinct regimes.

Starting from the initial one, the system behaved as \emph{ballistic}, for which the mean squared displacement (MSD) scales quadratically with time: $\langle \Delta r^2(t)\rangle \;\approx\; v_0^2\,t^2  $
where $v_0$ is the self-propulsion speed or motility and $\alpha = 2$. 
The latter one was \emph{diffusive} regime, for which the MSD scales linearly with time:
$  \langle \Delta r^2(t)\rangle \;\approx\; v_0\,t $.

The early-time motility coefficient $v_0$ was quantified by fitting short-time data to the power-law model
$ (\Delta r)^2 \approx v_0^\alpha\,(\Delta t)^\alpha$.
Natural logarithms were taken, $ 2\ln(\Delta r) = \alpha\bigl[\ln(v_0)+\ln(\Delta t)\bigr]$ 
and division by 2 yielded $ \ln(\Delta r) = \beta_1\,\ln(\Delta t) + \beta_0 $ 
where $\beta_1=\alpha/2$ and $\beta_0=(\alpha/2),\ln(v_0)$. A linear least-squares regression was then applied and the 
%to
%$$ Y_i=\ln(\Delta r_i), \quad X_i=\ln(\Delta t_i)$$ 
%using the design matrix
%$$ X = \begin{pmatrix} 
%1 & X_1 \\ 1 & X_2 \\ 
%\vdots & \vdots \\ 
%1 & X_n 
%\end{pmatrix}, \quad \boldsymbol{\beta} = (X^T X)^{-1}X^T Y. $$ 
exponent and motility coefficient were obtained from
$ \alpha = 2 \beta_1$ and  $v_0 = \exp\bigl(2\beta_0/\alpha\bigr) = \exp\bigl(\beta_0/\beta_1\bigr)$. 
The correlation time $\tau$, defined as the point of intersection between ballistic and diffusive power laws, was calculated as:$ \tau = \exp\Bigl(\frac{\ln(v_{0,2}) - \ln(v_{0,1})}{\alpha_1 - \alpha_2}\Bigr) $,  where $(\alpha_1,v_{0,1})$ and $(\alpha_2,v_{0,2})$ were derived from separate log-log regressions on data segments partitioned at a crossover time $t_c$ chosen to minimize combined mean-squared error.
For each trajectory, values of $v_{0,i}$ and $\tau_i$ were compiled across the cell population, and bar plots of means with standard errors were generated to visualize their distributions.


\subsection{Elongation Analysis}
We used the phase contrast images corresponding to the fluorescence images to compute the elongation axis of the cells. In particular, a directional intensity analysis was implemented on bright‐field (BF) microscopy images via a custom Python pipeline to estimate the morphological elongation of individual cells. Nuclear fluorescence (RFP channel) was used to determine the centroid of each nucleus, from which the elongation axis was inferred. Evaluation was performed along a discrete set of orientations $\theta \in [0, \pi)$, uniformly spaced. For each direction, a linear segment of $\pm 150$ pixels (300 pixels in total) was sampled symmetrically from the centroid. The average BF intensity was computed along each segment.
In bright field imaging, cell boundaries typically produce high-intensity (bright) signals due to light scattering at refractive index gradients from the medium to the cell. Conversely, internal regions and elongated protrusions often present lower contrast and appear darker; the underlying assumption is that directions intersecting prominent cell boundaries will yield higher mean intensity values, whereas directions aligned with elongation, where the cell extends more continuously, will intersect fewer such transitions, resulting in lower average intensities.
Thus, the direction along which the average intensity is minimized is taken as the morphological axis of maximal extension. 
The method provides an axial orientation and is particularly suitable for detecting elongated geometries. 
To complement the intensity‐based orientation, a purely geometric analysis was performed on each cell.  Nuclei were manually segmented in the RFP channel and their centroids used to seed a watershed segmentation on the corresponding BF images, yielding one binary mask per cell.  Only masks that remained fully contained within the image frame at all time points were retained to avoid boundary artifacts.  The \texttt{skimage.measure.regionprops\_table} function was applied to each mask to extract geometric descriptors (area, perimeter, eccentricity).  The mask’s pixel coordinates $\{(x_i,y_i)\}_{i=1}^N$ were assembled into the $N\times 2$ matrix $X$ and subjected to principal‐component analysis via \texttt{sklearn.decomposition.PCA}, which diagonalizes the empirical covariance $C=\tfrac{1}{N}(X-\bar X)^\top(X-\bar X)$.  The first principal component, i.e.\ the eigenvector $\mathbf{d}=(d_x,d_y)$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue, defines the major axis of the ellipse that best fits the cell shape.
From the tracking pipeline, each cell’s instantaneous displacement vector at time $t$, $\mathbf{r}_t=(x_{t+1}-x_t,\;y_{t+1}-y_t)$, and its polar angle $\alpha_t=\operatorname{atan2}(r_{t,y},\,r_{t,x})$ were obtained. The geometric elongation‐axis angle was measured as $\beta=\operatorname{atan2}(d_y,\,d_x)$ from the same horizontal reference.  Having obtained both $\alpha_t$ and $\beta$, orientation alignment was quantified by the nematic difference $\Delta=|\alpha_t-\beta|$, which ignores sign and 180° reversals and was then folded into $[0,\tfrac{\pi}{2}]$ by setting $\Delta\leftarrow\min\{\Delta,\;2\pi-\Delta,\;\pi-\Delta\}$, ensuring that $\Delta$ always represents the acute angle between motion and shape, regardless of direction. 
To quantify the relationship between morphological and motile orientations, each tracked cell at each timepoint was characterized by three quantities: its per‐frame displacement magnitude $|\Delta \mathbf{r_t}|$, the alignment angle $\Delta\in[0,\tfrac{\pi}{2}]$ between motion and elongation axes, and its eccentricity $e$.  The objective was to determine how the mean displacement varies as a function of $\Delta$. First, $\Delta$ was partitioned into contiguous bins of width $\delta=0.2$ rad spanning the interval $[0,\pi]$.  For the $i$-th bin, defined by $[\Delta_i,\Delta_i+\delta)$, all displacement magnitudes $y_j=\|\Delta \mathbf{r_t}|_j$ whose corresponding $\Delta_j$ falls within that interval were collected into a vector $\mathbf{y}$, and their associated eccentricities into a weight vector $\mathbf{w}$. The weighted mean displacement in bin $i$ was then computed as  
$ \bar{y}_i = \frac{\sum_j w_j\,y_j}{\sum_j w_j}$, while the weighted variance was estimated by  
$ \sigma_i^2 = \frac{\sum_j w_j\,(y_j - \bar y_i)^2}{\sum_j w_j}$,  
and the corresponding standard error of the mean, where the eccentricity, used as weights, emphasizes cells with more pronounced elongation. 
%Finally, the “binned‐angle” curve was plotted by placing markers at the bin centers $\Delta_i + \tfrac{\delta}{2}$ with vertical coordinates $\bar{y}_i$ and error bars of length. This visualization highlights whether cells with smaller alignment angles (i.e.\ better alignment of motion and shape) exhibit systematically higher or lower displacement speeds.  


\subsection{Voronoi Tesselation and structure factor analysis}

Voronoi tessellations were generated from fluorescence microscopy images of nuclei by manually selecting each nucleus’s center and computing the corresponding Voronoi diagram using custom Python code built on the SciPy library. Regions of interest were manually segmented and saved as label images to isolate neural‐rosette formation within each field of view, and the coordinates of the selected nuclei centers within that ROI were loaded into the Voronoi function to produce a set of polygonal regions. A zero‐valued matrix matching the input image dimensions was initialized to store the target shape factor, defined as the ratio of a region’s perimeter to the square root of its area $S_{t}= \frac{p_i}{\sqrt{A_i}}$, at the appropriate pixel locations. Infinite Voronoi regions were discarded automatically, and only polygons entirely contained within the image boundaries were retained for analysis. 

To investigate the temporal evolution of cell morphology under identical culture conditions, we performed two parallel experiments—one in which neural rosette formation was observed and one in which it failed to occur. Fluorescence images were acquired daily from approximately day 6 through day 11 and cropped to a consistent 400 × 400-pixel ROI; however, significant differences between the two conditions only became apparent from day 8 onward. At each time point, nuclei centroids were identified and used to compute the target shape factor for each associated Voronoi region. 
%All per-cell shape factor measurements were stored in a single Pandas DataFrame alongside the label for the experimental condition (`rosette' vs. `no-rosette'), the day, and a unique cell index. After concatenating data from both experiments, we extracted the unique days and, for each condition, calculated the mean shape factor and its standard error. %These values were plotted using Matplotlib’s errorbar- function, overlaying the rosette (blue) and no-rosette (orange) time series. 



\subsubsection{Rosette identification}
Rosettes were manually identified analyzing the acquired mosaics. Note that while rosette emergence conditions are being understand, it is difficult to  predict where they will actually form. Thus, the detection of rosette emergence  in 2D cell coltures required the acquisition of large image mosaics that balance spatial resolution with extended field of view. This results in large mosaic images that must be manually analysed and hard to process and visualize. Future work should be done to address this technical issue using multiresolution data formats and visualization tools, e.g. the Cesium ion 3D tiles via ATON framework~\cite{fanini2021aton} which is part of the  the H2IOSC infrastructure.


