The space of partitions $\mathcal{P}$ is a partially ordered set, under the binary relation $\leq$ on $\mathcal{P}$ defined by set containment, i.e. for two partitions $\rho_1$ and $\rho_2$,  $\rho_1 \leq \rho_2$ if for every set $C_{1,j} \in \rho_1$, $C_{1,j} \subseteq C_{2,j'}$ for some set $C_{2,j'} \in \rho_2$. 

For any $\rho_1, \rho_2 \in \mathcal{P}$, $\rho_1$ is \textit{covered} by $\rho_2$, denoted $\rho_1 \prec \rho_2$, if $\rho_1 < \rho_2$ and there is no $ \rho_3 \in \mathcal{P}$ such that $ \rho_1 < \rho_3 < \rho_2$. This covering relation is used to define the \textit{Hasse diagram}, where the elements of $\mathcal{P}$ are represented as nodes of a graph and a line is drawn from $\rho_1$ up to $\rho_2$ when $\rho_1 \prec \rho_2$. An example of the Hasse diagram for $n=4$ is depicted in Figure \ref{fig:hasse_vi_N4}.

The space of partitions possesses an even richer structure; it forms a lattice. This follows from the fact that every pair of partitions has a \textit{greatest lower bound} and \textit{least upper bound}; for a subset $ \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{P}$, an element $\rho_1 \in \mathcal{P}$ is an upper bound for $ \mathcal{S}$ if $ \rho \leq \rho_1$ for all $\rho \in \mathcal{S}$, and $\rho_1 \in \mathcal{P}$ is the least upper bound for $ \mathcal{S}$, denoted $\rho_1=\text{l.u.b.}(\mathcal{S})$, if $\rho_1$ is an upper bound for $\mathcal{S}$ and $ \rho_1 \leq \rho'$ for all upper bounds $rho'$ of $\mathcal{S}$. A lower bound and the greatest lower bound  for a subset $ \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{P}$ are similarly defined, the latter denoted by $\text{g.l.b.}(\mathcal{S})$.

We define the operators $\wedge$, called the meet, and $\vee$, called the join, as $\rho_1 \wedge \rho_2 = \text{g.l.b.}(\rho_1,\rho_2)$ and  $\rho_1 \vee \rho_2=\text{l.u.b.}(\rho_1, \rho_2)$. Following the conventions of lattice theory, we will use $ \mathbf{1}$ to denote the greatest element of the lattice of partitions, i.e. the partition with every observation in one cluster $\rho=( \lbrace 1,\ldots,n \rbrace)$, and  $\mathbf{0}$ to denote the least element of the lattice of partitions, i.e. the partition with every observation in its own cluster $\rho = (\lbrace 1\rbrace,\ldots, \lbrace n \rbrace)$. See \cite{Nation91} for more details on lattice theory

\begin{definition}[Meet of the particles]
The meet of the particles $\rho_1^*,\ldots, \rho_L^*$, denoted by $\rho_{\text{meet}}$, is defined as the greatest lower bound of the particles, under the binary relation defined by set containment, where for two partitions $\rho_1$ and $\rho_2$,  $\rho_1 \leq \rho_2$ if for every set $C_{1,j} \in \rho_1$,  $C_{1,j} \subseteq C_{2,j'}$ for some set $C_{2,j'} \in \rho_2$. 
\end{definition}
Note that the meet can be found by considering the cross tabulation across all particles, and keeping only cases where there are positive counts. While in theory, there could be up to $K_1*\ldots*K_L$ clusters in the meet, where $K_l$ denotes the number of clusters in particle $\rho_l^*$, in practice there are significantly less.  




\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Slightly multimodal}

In this first study, data is generated from a mixture of normals that is slightly multimodal. This is motivated by \citet{rajkowski2019analysis}, who focuses on the behavior of the MAP clustering when using a DP mixture of normals. He shows that for a slightly bimodal example, the partition with a single large cluster has higher posterior mass than the intuitive clustering which partitions the data into groups based on positive and negative observed values. We consider a slightly more general setting with a mixture of three components. 


