\section{Discussion}

Understanding the uncertainty characterized by the posterior distribution is challenging in complex, high-dimensional Bayesian models. Additionally, as the posterior is intractable in such settings, algorithms for approximate inference, such as MCMC, are employed, which can return tens to hundreds of thousands of approximate posterior draws.  In this work, we focus on clustering, where the posterior is defined on the high-dimensional, partially-ordered space of partitions. In this non-standard setting, we propose a WASserstein Approximation for Bayesian cluserIng (WASABI) to summarize the posterior draws with a small number of weighted partition estimates. Through extensive simulations and analyses, we showcase its utility in practical settings when some clusters are not well separated or the model may be misspecified. Indeed, while many applications focus on a single clustering estimate, we demonstrate how it can be misleading in such settings and how WASABI captures the different modes of clustering. 

The Wasserstein approximation requires selecting a metric on the partition space. We focus on the variation of information \citep{Meila07}, also exploiting its properties to propose novel tools to assess uncertainty in the cluster assignment of individual points. However, this can be easily extended to other choices, such as Binder's loss, ARI, or generalized versions \citep{dahl2022search}, or to more general settings, e.g. \cite{buch2024bayesian} develop a Bayesian approach to clustering in the presence of noise. 

An efficient algorithm, along with various tools to visualize and quantify uncertainty, is provided in the R package at \url{https://github.com/cecilia-balocchi/WASABI}. In practice, only a small number of iterations are typically required, with each one scaling linearly with the number of data points and MCMC samples. To reduce the cost of the latter, WASABI allows subsampling of the MCMC draws. For the former, future work will explore combining WASABI with distributed methods for Bayesian clustering \citep{ni2020scalable}. 

WASABI is useful for improved understanding of the posterior, but could also be relevant for posterior compression in order to reduce both storage (saving only the WASABI particles and weights) and the computational cost of predictive inference. For example, density estimation can be expensive, depending on the size of the grid or number of new locations. For Example~\ref{ex:2modes1d}, density estimates are provided in Section \ref{app:density_ex1} of the Appendix, where the WASABI-based estimates significantly  reduce the cost by a factor of $1,000$ compared to MCMC, at only a small increase in error. 

Beyond clustering, the Wasserstein approximation could be beneficial for other Bayesian models. Future work, will investigate settings, such as variable selection, trees, networks, and more.  

%\SW{We can also motivate as posterior compression and reducing storage, e.g. to avoid saving all the posterior samples. In this case, maybe we want to show that inference, e.g. density estimation and uncertainty is still good in experiments? }


%\SW{An extension could be to combine with ideas in \cite{ni2020scalable} for scalable clustering. Instead of freezing clusters with a point estimate for each subset, we could allow some uncertainty and freeze within each particle...}

%\SW{Mention something here about BALLET?} BALLET \citep{buch2024bayesian} provides an interesting alternative approach to Bayesian clustering, that separates the tasks of density estimation and clustering. Specifically, MCMC draws of the clustering are not based on the mixture allocation vector directly, but instead are obtained from a level set of corresponding draw of the density. Moreover, this allows extending Bayesian clustering to any Bayesian model for density estimation (i.e. not only mixtures but also methods such as Polya trees \citep{lavine1992some,wong2010optional} and logistic Gaussian processes \citep{lenk1991towards,tokdar2007towards}). %, MCMC draws of the clustering allocation vector are obtained based on a level set of corresponding draw of the density.  
%In BALLET, each MCMC level-set clustering may contain \textit{noise points}, which are not allocated to any cluster. Thus, our proposal can also be relevant to describe uncertainty in the MCMC draws of BALLET clustering, using suitable extensions of loss functions to account for noise points (e.g. the inactive-active Binder's loss \citep{buch2024bayesian}). 


