\address{Institut de Recherche Mathématique Avancée (IRMA), Strasbourg 67084, France}
\email{soumyajit.saha@unistra.fr}


\begin{abstract} In this note, we investigate the effects of metric perturbations of closed surfaces on the number of nodal domains. In particular, we show that the nodal domain count for Laplace eigenfunctions
is (locally) maximised for non-generic metrics. We also investigate localised perturbations and showcase some interesting applications regarding Courant sharp metrics on closed surfaces and associated boundary nodal-data prescription problems.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle


\section{Introduction and main results} Let $(M, g)$ be a closed Riemannian surface.  Consider the eigenequation
\begin{equation}\label{eqtn: Eigenfunction equation}
    -\Delta \varphi = \lambda \varphi, %\hspace{2mm} i=1, 2, \cdots,
\end{equation}
where $\Delta$ is the Laplace-Beltrami operator given by (using the Einstein summation convention) 
$$
\Delta f = \frac{1}{\sqrt{|g|}}\pa_i \left( \sqrt{|g|}g^{ij}\pa_j f\right),
$$
where $|g|$ is the determinant of the metric tensor $g_{ij}$. In the Euclidean space, this reduces to the usual $\Delta = \pa_1^2 + \dots + \pa_n^2$ (note that we are using the analyst's sign convention for the Laplacian, namely that $-\Delta$ is positive semidefinite).  
Recall that  
the Laplacian $-\Delta$ has a discrete spectrum 
$$ 
0\leq\lambda_1 \leq \dots \leq \lambda_k \leq \dots \nearrow \infty,
$$
repeated with multiplicity with corresponding (real-valued $L^2$ normalised) eigenfunctions $\varphi_k$, which form a complete orthonormal basis of $L^2(M)$.  

Let  $\mathcal{N}(\varphi) = \{ x \in M: \varphi (x) = 0\}$ denote the nodal set of the eigenfunction $\varphi$. On a closed $n$-manifold, the nodal set is the union of a smooth hypersurface and a set that is countably $(n - 2)$-rectifiable (see \cite{Cheng1976, HardtSimon1989, Baer1997}). In particular, for $n =2$, the nodal set is a disjoint  union of finitely many embedded open arcs and finitely many critical points where the nodal set self-intersects. %Sometimes for ease of notation, we will also denote the nodal set by $\NNN(\varphi_\lambda)$. 
Recall that any connected component of $M \setminus \mathcal{N}(\varphi)$ is known as a nodal domain of the eigenfunction $\varphi$, and we generally denote nodal domains by $\Omega$ (with minor abuse of notation). Let us denote by $\nu(\varphi)$ the number of nodal domains for $\varphi$ and by $\mathcal{S}(\varphi)$ the set of self-intersection points (termed as \textit{nodal critical points}) of $\varphi$. The latter contained in $\mathcal{C}(\varphi)$, the set of all critical points of $\varphi$. Observe that the set $\Cal{C}(\varphi)$ does not need to be isolated; in fact, it can be a hypersurface, as given by the eigenfunction $\sin(2k\pi x)$ on $[0, 1]_x \times [0, 1]_y$. However, $\nabla \varphi$ satisfies an elliptic system for which one can establish Carleman estimates and unique continuation (see, for example, \cite{Laurent2012}), due to which $\Cal{C}$ cannot have an interior point. It  follows from the maximum principle that eigenfunctions are either strictly positive or strictly negative on each nodal domain, and change signs across non-critical nodal points.  


One of the intriguing areas of research in spectral geometry is the study of nodal domain counting. This topic has been extensively explored over the past century, with one of the earliest significant results due to Courant \cite{Courant1923} where he established an upper bound on the number of nodal domains associated with an eigenfunction $\varphi_k$, namely,  $\nu(\varphi_k)\leq k$. Shortly thereafter, Stern \cite{Ste} (see also \cite{BH}), in a somewhat opposite vein, demonstrated the existence of a sequence of eigenfunctions on a rectangle, each containing exactly two nodal domains. A similar result was also proved  in \cite{Lewy} where it was shown that %they considered a 2-sphere and proved that 
there exists a sequence of eigenfunctions $\{\varphi_k\}$ with $\lambda_k\to \infty$ on the usual round sphere $S^2$ such that $\nu(\varphi_k)\leq 3$ for every $k$. The results of Lewy and Stern focus on the construction of eigenfunctions with the minimal possible number of nodal domains.  We also have results that explore the opposite direction. From the recent results of \cite{ghosh2013nodal, jung2016number, jung2018quantum}, we see that under the assumption of quantum ergodicity and certain restrictions on compact hyperbolic/modular surfaces, we have that $\nu(\varphi_k)\to \infty$ as $\lambda\to \infty$. So a naturally related question in this area is the study of Courant-sharp eigenvalues and eigenfunctions. We refer to $\varphi_k$ as Courant-sharp if $\nu(\varphi_k)=k$. Pleijel \cite{Pleijel1956} showed that in the Dirichlet case for domains in $\RR^2$, only finitely many eigenfunctions can be Courant-sharp. This result was further generalised to compact Riemannian manifolds in higher dimensions in the works of \cite{peetre1957generalization, berard1982inegalites, polterovich2009pleijel}. There is also some previous work towards finding formulae for nodal domain counting which are roughly based on Euler characteristic arguments; for example, see \cite{Hoffmann-OstenhofMichorNadirashvili1999} for the case of planar bounded domains with smooth boundary.  These results, among many others, highlight the intricacies of nodal domain counting, a topic that incorporates a wide range of mathematical tools and techniques in its study. 


In this article, our aim is to study the effect of metric perturbations on nodal domain count. It is known that nodal sets are not stable under generic perturbations (see e.g. \cite{Uhlenbeck1976}). Thus it is expected that the number of nodal domains would also change under perturbations.  
In \cite{BeckGuptaMarzuola2024-rectangle},\cite{Lyons2025-rectangle}, the authors gave a precise description of nodal structure upon perturbation of rectangular domains under the simplicity assumption of the corresponding eigenvalue; they have shown that the number of nodal domains decreases under the perturbation of rectangles. To the best of our knowledge, the behaviour of the number of nodal domains for general manifold under arbitrary perturbations has not been discussed previously, and our investigations are geared towards this. From well-known numerics and also from a heuristic standpoint, it is believed that the nodal intersection picture becomes generally less complicated  on %{\em non-generic} 
perturbation; this is because (heuristically speaking), on perturbation the nodal set ``untwines''. At the background of our considerations is the following fact (see \cite{Uhlenbeck1976,Albert1973}): a Laplace eigenfunction for a generic metric (in the sense of Baire category) is a Morse function and also has no nodal critical points. So, generically, the nodal set is a collection of embedded circles, and every critical point is either a point of extremum or a saddle point. 



We work with the following setup: consider a closed Riemannian surface  
and a sequence of smooth metrics $g_t$ on $M$ such that $g_t \to g_0$ and a corresponding smooth one-parameter family of Laplace eigenfunctions such that 
\beq\label{eq:eigenf_conv}
\varphi_t \longrightarrow \varphi_0 \text{  in  } C^\infty-\text{topology},
\eeq

where $\varphi_0$ automatically becomes a Laplace eigenfunction for $(M, g_0)$. One typically convenient setting is where $(M, g_0)$ has simple spectrum, which automatically produces a smooth eigenfunction branch converging to each eigenfunction of $(M, g_0)$ (for more details on this, see the very general formulation outlined in \cite{KriegenMichorRainer11}).

Our first main result discusses the local behaviour of nodal critical points and nodal domains under generic perturbations. Among other things, our study leads to  an estimate on the number of ``local nodal domains'' around a nodal critical point after perturbation. Formally, this means the number of connected components $F_t(p)$ of $\displaystyle(M \setminus \NNN(\varphi_t) )\cap B_{g_0}(p, r)$, where $p$ is a nodal critical point of $\varphi_0$. We spell out clearly that for all our perturbative results, distances and other metric properties are with respect to a fixed background metric which is ``close'' to $g_t$ for small $t$. In particular, this background metric can be $g_0$. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:loc_nod_est}  
Let $p\in M$ be a nodal critical point of $\varphi_0$ with order of vanishing $k$. Also, denote by $C_t$ the number of connected components of $\NNN(\varphi_t)\cap B(p, r)$. %Then we have the following. 
Given $r$ small enough, there exists $\varepsilon := \varepsilon(r) > 0$ such that for any $|t| < \varepsilon$, the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(a)] The number of nodal critical points of $\varphi_t$ contained inside $B(p,r)$ does not exceed $k-1$.

    \item[(b)] The order of vanishing of any nodal critical point of $\varphi_t$ within $B(p, r)$ cannot be greater than $k$.

    \item[(c)]
    We have the following estimate:
    $$
    F_t(p) = F_0(p)-C_t+1.
    $$
    In particular, $F_0(p) \geq F_t(p) $.
\end{enumerate}

\end{theorem}

Using the above local estimates, we get the following global version. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main_result_A}
    The number of nodal domains cannot decrease in the limit. More precisely, whenever \eqref{eq:eigenf_conv} holds, we have that
    \beq\label{ineq:nodal_domain_decrease}
     \nu(\varphi_t) \leq \nu(\varphi_0) 
    \eeq 
    for small enough $|t|$. In particular, if $\varphi_0$ has no nodal critical point, then $\nu(\varphi_t) = \nu(\varphi_0)$.
\end{theorem}
It is interesting to observe that in the above set up, the difference $\nu(\varphi_0) - \nu(\varphi_t)$ is controlled from above by the indices of $\nabla\varphi_0$ and $\nabla \varphi_t$ at their nodal critical points. Such an estimate has been outlined in Corollary \ref{cor:nod_dom_dec_est}, which is complementary to \eqref{ineq:nodal_domain_decrease} above. 


Observe that till now we have been dealing with metric perturbations, but the topology stayed the same throughout the perturbation. In Section \ref{sec:changing_topology}, we prove  Theorem \ref{thm:perturb_nodal_domain_reduction}, which is a counterpart of Theorem \ref{thm:main_result_A} above, but in % Now, we make a few comments addressing 
the case of perturbations which are local in nature but allows for change in the topology of the surface. These types of perturbation have been studied extensively (see \cite{Takahashi2002,Kom2005,MukherjeeSaha2022} and references therein). This has interesting consequences. For instance, as an application of  Theorem \ref{thm:perturb_nodal_domain_reduction}, 
we prove that on any given closed surface, there exists Riemannian metrics which support  Courant-sharp Laplace eigenfunctions up to arbitrary (but of course finite) level. We refer the reader to \cite{BerardHelffer2016-Courant_sharp_1,  Bonnaillie-NoelHelffer2016, BerardHelfferKiwan-Courant_sharp_2, BerardHelfferKiwan2022-Courant_sharp_3} for previous results on more specific manifolds. We state the result formally.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:Freitas_app}
     Given any two positive integers $k, m$, one can find a closed Riemannian surface $(M, g)$ of genus $m$ such that %the first $k$ eigenvalues are simple, and 
     for every $l\leq k$ the corresponding Laplace eigenfunction $\varphi_l$ of $-\Delta_g$ has exactly $l$ nodal domains, i.e., $\nu(\varphi_l)=l$ for all $l\leq k$. 
\end{theorem}


\begin{remark}
    Observe that one can generically perturb the above obtained metric $g$, and the perturbed manifold $(M, g_\epsilon)$ also satisfies the Courant-sharp property as in Theorem \ref{thm:Freitas_app}, and in addition can be of simple spectrum. This can be readily seen by applying Theorem \ref{thm:main_result_A} to $(M,g)$. In other words, the first $k$ eigenfunctions of any closed Riemannian surface $M$ are Courant-sharp if the metric lies within a small neighbourhood of $g$ constructed in Theorem \ref{thm:Freitas_app}. 
\end{remark}

Now we look at our final result, which deals with the prescription of the number of nodal intersections on the boundary of a surface. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: prescription_nodal_intersection_multiple_bdry}
    Let $M$ be a Riemannian surface with genus $m$ and boundary components $B_1, \cdots, B_b$, which are topological circles. Given $n_1, \cdots, n_b \in \mathbb{N}$, there exists a metric $g$ on $M$ such that for some $l\in \NN$ the nodal set of Neumann eigenfunctions $\varphi_{l+i}$ intersects the boundary $B_i$ at exactly $2n_i$ points where $i\in \{1, \cdots, b\}$. %Moreover, the nodal set of $u_{l+i}$ does not intersect any other boundary component.  
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Organisation of the paper}\label{subsec:plan} Now we take the space to outline the overall plan of the paper. In Section \ref{Sec: Preliminary results}, we prove technical lemmata on certain stability properties of indices of $\nabla \varphi_t$ and nodal critical points of $\varphi$, and also make some preliminary (non-perturbative) observations about the structure of nodal critical points. Among other things, we show that nodal critical points have negative index, are isolated in the set of critical points, and cannot bifurcate/split too fast on perturbation. As a preliminary result, we prove that in the absence of nodal critical points, the nodal domain count remains unchanged upon perturbation (Proposition \ref{prop:nodal_line_no_crossing}). The ideas of the proof will be crucial in studying the behaviour of nodal sets away from the nodal critical points in the general setting and in making the transition from Theorem \ref{thm:loc_nod_est} to Theorem \ref{thm:main_result_A}. 
 In Section \ref{Sec: Perturbation and critical points}, we discuss a local stability property of $\NNN(\varphi_t)$ under small perturbations as asserted in Lemma \ref{lem:nod_lin_disc_invariant}, namely, we prove that $|\NNN(\varphi_t) \cap \pa B(p,r)|$ remains invariant for small $t$. Using this, we prove our first main result stated in Theorem \ref{thm:loc_nod_est}. In Section \ref{Sec: Proofs of main theorem 2}, we prove our second main result stated in Theorem \ref{thm:main_result_A}. In the same section, we see that the decrement in the number of nodal domains is directly related to the change of the total index of nodal critical points upon perturbation; this is recorded in Corollary \ref{cor:nod_dom_dec_est}. In Section \ref{sec:changing_topology}, we consider perturbations which are localised in a small region but allow for change in the topology there (see the discussion at the beginning of the section). We quickly outline how to prove a useful analogue of Theorem \ref{thm:main_result_A} geared towards such perturbations; this is Theorem  \ref{thm:perturb_nodal_domain_reduction}. As interesting applications of the latter, we prove  Theorem \ref{thm:Freitas_app} and Theorem \ref{thm: prescription_nodal_intersection_multiple_bdry}. Lastly, in an Appendix, we include a brief proof of the fact that inner radius estimates for nodal domains stay locally controlled in the settings that we consider. We have used this fact implicitly in the main body of the text several times.%\SSa{@SM, complete this! needs to be improved}


\section{Preliminary results}\label{Sec: Preliminary results}
\subsection{Stability of isolated critical points under perturbation} 
Let $\mathfrak{X}^1(M)$ denote the Banach space of $C^1$-vector fields on $M$. Let us denote the set of all zeros of $X\in \mathfrak{X}^1(M)$ by $\CCC$. For a vector field $X$ and an {\em isolated zero} $p\in \CCC$, we use $\ind_p(X)$ to denote the index of the vector field $X$ at $p$.  Later we shall extend the notion of index to any set of zeros of $X$. Throughout the following discussion, any open neighbourhood of a set $\tilde{\CCC} \subset \CCC$ that we denote by $U$ is implicitly assumed to have the property that $X \neq 0$ on $\overline{U} \setminus \tilde{\CCC}$ and also implicitly assumed to be diffeomorphic to the Euclidean disc. Any connected open subset of $U$ containing $\tilde{\CCC}$ is assumed to have the same properties. We use the fact that in dimension $2$, the index of an isolated zero can be measured by the winding number of the curve $X \circ \gamma$ around $0$ where $\gamma$ is any parametrisation of $\pa U$. %Henceforth, it is understood that whenever we refer to the index of a critical point, we assume the latter to be an isolated critical point. 
We have the following proposition:
  \begin{proposition}\label{prop: winding number is constant}
    Let $X_t \in \mathfrak{X}^1(M)$ be a 1-parameter family of vector fields on $M$ such that the map $t \mapsto X_t \in \mathfrak{X}^1(M)$ %\in C^1([a,b], \mathfrak{X}^1(M))$. %C^{1}(M,\CC))$ 
    %is continuous in $t$ with respect $C^1-$norm.
    is $C^1$ at $t_0 \in \RR$.
    Let $p \in M$ be an isolated zero of $X_{t_0}$ and $U$ be a neighbourhood of $p$ (as mentioned in the beginning of this section). For a smooth parametrisation $\gamma$ of $\pa U$,  the winding number $w(t)$ of $X_t \circ \gamma$ around $0$ is a continuous function at $t_0$, and hence, locally constant. %\mnote{We need to be careful about continuous ``near'' vs continuous ``at''.}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof} 
     It is sufficient to prove that the function
    \begin{equation*}
        t \mapsto w(t)=\frac{1}{2\pi} \oint_{X_t \circ \gamma} \frac{dz}{z}.
    \end{equation*}
   is continuous at $t_0$. Let $\eta_t: \SSS^1 \to \CC \setminus \{0\}$, defined by $\eta_t= X_t \circ \gamma$, be a $C^1$ family of closed curves. Then the above integral can be re-written as 
   \begin{equation*}
       w(t) = \int_0^1 \frac{\eta_t' (s) ds}{\eta_t(s)}.
   \end{equation*}
   Let $t_n$ be a sequence converging to $t_0$. Since $(s, t) \to \eta_t(s),\eta_t'(s)$ are continuous on the compact set $\{t_0\} \times \SSS^1$, $|\eta_{t_n}(s)|, |\eta'_{t_n}(s)|$ have uniform maximum and minimum values on compact sets, i.e. $0 < m<|\eta_{t_n}(s)|<M$ and $|\eta_{t_n}'(s)| < M_1$ for all $s \in  \SSS^1, n \geq N$ for some $N \in \NN$. By the dominated convergence theorem $|w(t_n)-w(t_0)| \to 0$ as $n\to \infty$. Thus $w$ is an integer valued function, continuous at $t_0$,  which in turn implies that it is continuous in a small interval containing $t_0$.
\end{proof}
 Let $p_1, \dots, p_n$ be a finite collection of isolated zeros of a $C^1$ vector field $X$, all contained in an open neighbourhood $U$. Then, the {\em total index} of $X$ at the set $\tilde{\CCC}:=\{p_i:i=1,\cdots,n\} \subset \CCC$ is defined to be the sum $\sum_{j = 1}^n \ind_{p_j} (X)=: \ind_{\tilde{\CCC}}(X)$.  A simple observation reveals that the total index of $X$ at $\tilde{\CCC}$ is given by the winding number of $X \circ \gamma$ around $0$, where $\gamma $ is any smooth parametrisation of $\pa U$. Indeed, if we introduce the curves $\gamma_i$ with the same orientation as $\gamma$ whose closure only contains $p_i$ and no other zeros of $X$, the winding number of $X \circ \gamma$ around $0$ is equal to the sum of the winding numbers of $X \circ \gamma_i$ around $0$. Now, the conclusion follows from the identification of $\ind_{p_i}(X)$ with the winding number of $X \circ \gamma_i$ around $0$.




The set of zeros of a vector field is not necessarily discrete in general, e.g. when $X=\nabla \varphi$ for a Laplace eigenfunction $\varphi$ (as mentioned in the introduction). We cannot define the index of a vector field %on an open neighbourhood containing 
at a set of non-isolated zeros in the same way as the isolated case. However,   \cite[Section 1.1.2]{Brasselet87} has prescribed  a very interesting way to define the total index of a vector field at any set of zeros. We quote a result below:
%We say that the vector field $X$ is non-singular on an open set $V \subset M$ if $X(q) \neq 0$ for all $q \in V$. 
\begin{theorem}\cite[Theorem 1.1.2.]{Brasselet87}\label{thm: extended vector field -total index}
Let $U \subset M$ be a domain with boundary $\pa U$ and $X\in \mathfrak{X}^1(M)$ such that $X \neq 0$ in a neighbourhood $V$ of $\pa U$. Then
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $X$ can be extended to the interior of $U$ with finitely many isolated zeros $p_1,\dots,p_r$.
    \item The total index of the extension (in the sense described two paragraphs above) is independent of the way we extend it to the interior of $U$ from $V$. In other words, if $\tilde{X}_1$ and $\tilde{X}_2$ are two extensions of $X$ with corresponding finite sets of isolated zeros $\tilde{\mathcal{C}}_1$ and $\tilde{\mathcal{C}}_2$ respectively, then $\ind_{\tilde{\mathcal{C}}_1}(\tilde{X}_1)=\ind_{\tilde{\mathcal{C}}_2}(\tilde{X}_2)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}


Consider a connected component $\tilde{\mathcal{C}}$ of the set of zeros $\mathcal{C}$ of the vector field $X$. %{\color{red}
Let $\mathcal{T}$ be the \textit{cellular tubular neighbourhood} of $\tilde{\mathcal{C}}$ with smooth boundary as defined in \cite[Section 1.1.2]{Brasselet87}. We note that for any open neighbourhood $U$ of $\tilde{\mathcal{C}}$, there exists a cellular tubular neighbourhood $\mathcal{T}$ such that $\tilde{\mathcal{C}}\subset \mathcal{T} \subset U$. We always assume that a neighbourhood $U$ of a set of zeros is tubular and it satisfies $\mathcal{C}\cap U = \tilde{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\mathcal{C} \cap \pa U=\emptyset$. The vector field $X$ can be extended (call it $\tilde{X}$) to the interior of $U$ with finitely many isolated zeros $\{p_1,p_2,...,p_n\}$. As defined in \cite[Definition 1.1.3]{Brasselet87}, the \textit{total index} of the vector field $X$ at $\tilde{\mathcal{C}}$ is %defined to be 
the sum $ \ind_{\tilde{\mathcal{C}}}(X):=\sum_{i=1}^n \ind_{p_i}(\tilde{X})$. Since the sum is independent of the choice of the extension $\tilde{X}$ by Theorem \ref{thm: extended vector field -total index}, the definition of total index is well-defined. By a similar argument as in the isolated case, it is apparent that the total index of $X$ at any connected component $\tilde{\CCC}$ of the zero set $\CCC$ is given by the winding number of $X \circ \gamma$ at $0$, where $\gamma$ is any parametrisation of $\pa U$.  So, we have the following generalisation of Proposition \ref{prop: winding number is constant}:

\begin{corollary}\label{cor: winding number is constant, non-isolated}
    Let $X_t \in \mathfrak{X}^1(M)$ be a 1-parameter family of vector fields on $M$ such that the map $t \mapsto X_t \in \mathfrak{X}^1(M)$ is $C^1$ at $t_0 \in \RR$ and $\mathcal{C}_t$ be the corresponding set of zeros.
    Let $\tilde{\mathcal{C}}_{t_0}$ be a finite union of connected components of $\mathcal{C}_{t_0} \subset M$ and $U$ be an open neighbourhood of $\tilde{\mathcal{C}}_{t_0}$. For a smooth parametrisation $\gamma$ of $\pa U$,  the winding number $w(t)$ of $X_t \circ \gamma$ around $0$ is a continuous function at $t_0$, and hence, $\ind_{\tilde{\mathcal{C}}_t \cap U}(X_t)$ is locally constant. 
\end{corollary}



We apply the above %proposition and the lemma 
to eigenfunctions of the Laplacian on a 1-parameter family of smooth Riemannian manifolds $M_t := (M, g_t), t\in \RR$. %, {\color{blue}$C^1$ at $t_0$}. {\color{blue}}\mnote{Is $C^1$ enough, or we need more regularity?}. 
In what follows, we assume without any loss of generality that $t_0 = 0$ for convenience of notation.  
We now have the following proposition:
\begin{proposition} \label{prop: stability of critical points}
    Let $p$ be an isolated critical point of $\varphi_{0}$ with $\ind_p(\nabla \varphi_{0})$ positive (respectively, negative). For each neighbourhood $U$ of $p$, there exists $\epsilon>0$ such that if $|t|<\epsilon$ the perturbed eigenfunction $\varphi_t$ has a set of critical point in $U$ whose total index is positive (respectively, negative).
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}

 Since $\ind_p(\nabla \varphi_0) \neq 0$, we get that the winding number of the curve $\nabla \varphi_0 \circ \gamma$ around $0$ is non-zero. An application of Corollary \ref{cor: winding number is constant, non-isolated} to $X_t= \nabla \varphi_t$ implies that the winding number of $\nabla \varphi_t \circ \gamma$ around $0$ is a non-zero constant for $t\in [-\epsilon, \epsilon]$. Now, the proof follows by contradiction. If $\varphi_t$ has no critical point inside $U$, then $\frac{1}{z}$ is holomorphic in the closure of the curve $\nabla \varphi_t \circ \gamma (\SSS^1)$, giving zero winding number, a contradiction. 
 Therefore $\varphi_t$ must have a set critical points in $U$ with the non-zero total index and matching sign as $\ind_p(\nabla\varphi_0)$.
\end{proof}
\color{black}

Generally, it is possible to have a smooth vector field $X$ with an isolated zero $p$ such that $\ind_p(X) = 0$. For example, consider the vector field $(x^2 + y^2, 0)$ on $\RR^2$ which as an isolated zero at the origin with zero index. However, due to the sign changing properties of Laplace eigenfunctions across nodal domains, one has the following observations:
\begin{obsv}\label{obsv:2.3}
    At any isolated saddle point of a Laplace eigenfunction $\varphi$, $\nabla \varphi$ has negative index. At an isolated  point of maximum or minimum of $\varphi$, $\nabla \varphi$ has positive index.
\end{obsv}
Observe that in general a nodal critical point is not a saddle point, as $\varphi$ could have a higher order of vanishing there. However, in a somewhat related vein, one has the following:
\begin{obsv}\label{obsv:2.4}
   %Using Lemma 3.1 of \cite{alessandrini1992}, we have that 
   At any nodal critical point of a Laplace eigenfunction $\varphi$, $\nabla \varphi$ has negative index. More precisely, if $k$ is the order of vanishing of $\varphi$ at a nodal critical point $p$, then $\ind_p(\nabla \varphi) = 1-k$.
\end{obsv}

The last statement implicitly assumes that nodal critical points are isolated in the set of all critical points. This can be justified in light of the following proposition. Note that the latter part of the  proposition is contained in \cite{Cheng1976}. %Furthermore, we have the following
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:nod_crt_isol}
    Any nodal critical point $p$ of a Laplace eigenfunction $\varphi$ is isolated in the set of critical points of $ \varphi$. In particular, the number of nodal critical points is finite.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    Suppose $\varphi$ vanishes to order $k$ at $p$. We would like to show that in the set of all critical points of $\varphi$, $p$ is isolated. If not, then given any $\epsilon$ (however small), there exists a sequence of %{\color{blue}non-nodal} 
    critical points %\footnote{$x$ is a non-nodal critical point of $\varphi$ if $\varphi(x)\neq 0$ and $\nabla \varphi(x)=0$.} 
    $\{p_n\}\subset B(p, \epsilon)$ that converges to $p$. %, where $B(p, \epsilon)$ denotes a ball of radius $\epsilon$ centred at $p$.

    Now recall the following Taylor series expansion (see \cite{Bers1955}) near $p$ in polar coordinates
    \begin{equation}\label{eqn: Taylor series expansion eigenfunction}
        \varphi(r, \theta) = c_kr^k\cos k\theta + \sum_{j = k + 1}^\infty c_jr^j\cos j\theta,
    \end{equation}
    % {\color{red}We forgot the constants in the expansion!!}
    where $r=0$ denotes the point $p$. From our assumption, since $p_n$ is also a critical point contained in a small neighbourhood of $p$, we have
   \begin{align}
        \pa_\theta \varphi (p_n)=0 & \implies -r_n^k\left( kc_k \sin k\theta_n + r\sum^\infty_{j=k+1}jc_jr_n^{j-k-1}\sin j\theta_n\right)=0,\label{eqn: Taylor series eigenfunction gradient 1}\\
        \pa_r \varphi (p_n)=0 & \implies r_n^{k-1}\left( k c_k\cos k\theta_n +r\sum^\infty_{j=k+1}jc_jr_n^{j-k-1}\cos j\theta_n \right)=0,\label{eqn: Taylor series eigenfunction gradient 2}
    \end{align}
where $(r_n, \theta_n)$ denotes the corresponding polar coordinates of $p_n$. Note that for each $p_n$, we have that $r_n\neq 0$. We now prove that the series in the preceding equations are absolutely convergent and the radius of convergence is independent of $\theta$.  Since the remainder term in the Taylor series expansion \eqref{eqn: Taylor series expansion eigenfunction} of $\varphi$ satisfies $R_N(r,\theta)=O(r^{N+1})$, the radius of absolute convergence of the series $\sum_{j = k + 1}^\infty c_j r^j \cos j\theta_n$  is bounded below by a positive number, say $r_0$, independent of $\theta$. For example, a natural choice for $r_0$ is the radius of convergence of  $S_1:=\sum_{j = k + 1}^\infty |c_j| r^j$. 
By a similar analogy, the radius of convergence of $S_2:=\sum_{j = k + 1}^\infty j|c_j| s^{j-k-1}$ provides an uniform lower bound on the the radii of convergence of the series in the equations \eqref{eqn: Taylor series eigenfunction gradient 1}, \eqref{eqn: Taylor series eigenfunction gradient 2} for all $\theta$. We note that $S_2$ and $S_3=\sum_{j = k + 1}^\infty |c_j| s^{j-k-1}$ have same radius of convergence.  
We choose $l\in \NN$ such that after a reparametrisation $s=r^l$ we have $S_3 \leq S_1$, i.e.
$$s^{j-k-1} \leq r^j \iff  r^{l(j-k-1)} \leq r^j.$$
Since $r<1$ %it is sufficient to choose $l(j-k-1)>j$. Since 
and $\frac{j}{j-k-1} \nearrow 1$, it is sufficient to choose $l \in (\frac{1}{2},1)$. Therefore $S_3$, hence $S_2$, has radius of convergence at least $r_0^{\frac{1}{l}}$. Hence the series in  \eqref{eqn: Taylor series eigenfunction gradient 1}, \eqref{eqn: Taylor series eigenfunction gradient 2} are absolutely convergent with a non-zero uniform lower bound on the radii of convergence. Which in turn implies that $\cos k\theta_n = O_k(r_n)$ and $\sin k\theta_n = O_k(r_n)$.
Hence the first part of the proposition follows. The second part is a direct consequence of the compactness of $M$.
\end{proof}

We now illustrate the stability of nodal critical points under small perturbations by proving that the set of nodal critical points $\mathcal{S}_t:=\mathcal{S}(\varphi_t)$ of $\varphi_t$ remains close to $\mathcal{S}(\varphi_0)$. %the nodal critical points of $\varphi_0$. 
In other words,  
    \begin{proposition}\label{prop: stability - general}
    The set of nodal critical points is stable under perturbation in the following sense: if $p_1, \dots, p_n$ are the nodal critical points of $\varphi_0$, then given $r>0$, there exists $\delta>0$ (depending on $r$) such that 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{S}_t \subset \bigcup_{i=1}^n B(p_i,r) \subset M \quad \text{ whenever } |t|<\delta.
\end{equation*}
\end{proposition}
    \begin{proof}
   Since the nodal critical points are finite and discrete, there exists $r>0$ such that for any $p_i\in \mathcal{S}_0$, the ball $\overline{B(p_i,r)}$ does not contain any critical points of $\varphi_0$. %Given $r>0$ such that $\bigcup_{i=1}^n B(p_i,r) \subset M$ 
    Define $M'= M\setminus \bigcup_{i=1}^n B(p_i,r)$. We want to show that $\varphi_t$ does not have any nodal critical points in $M'$ for sufficiently small $t$.
    %Denote $$\mathcal{C_t}=\{x\in M: \nabla \varphi_t (x)=0\}.$$
     We know that $M'$ does not contain any nodal critical points of $\varphi_0$, i.e., $\left(\NNN(\varphi_0)\cap \mathcal{C}_0\right)\cap M'=\emptyset$. Moreover, since $\NNN(\varphi_0)\cap M'$ and $\mathcal{C}_0\cap M'$ are compact in $M'$, we can separate them by two disjoint open neighbourhoods $U$ and $V$ respectively. Define $M_1':=M' \setminus U$ and $M'_2:= M' \setminus V$. Observe that $\varphi_0$ does not vanish on $M_1'$ and that $\nabla \varphi_0$ does not vanish on $M_2'$.  Recall that we have assumed that $\varphi_t$ converges to $\varphi_0$ in $C^k(M)$ topology for every $k\in \NN\cup \{0\}$. This gives us
\begin{align*}
   & \sup_{x\in M} |\varphi_t(x) -\varphi_0(x)| < \epsilon \quad \forall t \in (-\delta,\delta)\\
   \implies &|\varphi_t(x) - \varphi_0(x)| < \epsilon \quad \forall t \in (-\delta,\delta) \; \mbox{and} \; x\in M'_1\\
   \implies & |\varphi_0(x)|-\epsilon < |\varphi_t(x)| < |\varphi_0(x)|+\epsilon \quad \forall t \in (-\delta,\delta) \; \mbox{and} \; x\in M'_1.
\end{align*}
Since $\varphi_0$ is non-zero on the compact set $M_1'$, $|\varphi_0(x)| > 2m $ (say) on $M_1'$. Choosing $\epsilon = m$, we conclude that
\begin{equation*}
    |\varphi_t(x)| \geq m \quad \forall t \in (-\delta,\delta) \; \mbox{and} \; x\in M'_1.
\end{equation*}
Since the eigenfunctions $\varphi_t$ also converges to $\varphi_0$ in $C^1(M)$, the above argument can be applied to $\nabla \varphi_t$ on $M'_2$ and we have
\begin{align*}
     & \sup_{x\in M} \|\nabla\varphi_t(x) -\nabla\varphi_0(x)\| < \epsilon \quad \forall t \in (-\delta,\delta)\\
   \implies &\|\nabla\varphi_t(x) - \nabla\varphi_0(x)\| < \epsilon \quad \forall t \in (-\delta,\delta) \; \mbox{and} \; x\in M'_2\\
   \implies & \|\nabla\varphi_t\| \geq m~(\text{for some $m$}) \quad \forall~ t \in (-\delta,\delta) \; \mbox{and} \; x\in M_2'.
\end{align*}
From the above discussion we conclude that $|\varphi_t(x)|$ and $\|\nabla \varphi_t(x)\|$ can not vanish simultaneously on $M'$. Thus, all the nodal critical points of $\varphi_t$ must be contained inside $\bigcup_{i=1}^n B(p_i,r)$.
%{\color{red}(NOTE: The gradient convergence is used very loosely. It's fine if we want to do stuff on domains but on manifolds we have to double check if these computations go through)}
\end{proof}
   \begin{remark}\label{remark: mod_prop_2.6}
     In the proof of Proposition \ref{prop: stability - general} we can make $\delta$ uniform under small change in the radius $r$. In other words, for $\rho>0$ small enough, there exist $\delta$ such that 
   \begin{align*}
       &S_t \subset \cup_{i=1}^n B(p_i,\tilde{r}), \hspace{5mm} \forall r-\rho\leq \tilde{r}\leq r+\rho, |t| <\delta\\
       \iff & S_t \subset \cup_{i=1}^n B(p_i,r-\rho), \hspace{5mm} \forall |t| <\delta,
       \end{align*}
       where $\delta$ is dependent on $r$ and $\rho$ but it is independent of $\tilde{r}\in [r-\rho,r+\rho]$. The proof relies on the compactness of the tubular neighbourhood $N_\rho(r):=\cup_{i=1}^n\overline{B(p_i,r+\rho) \setminus B(p_i,r-\rho)}$ of the boundary $\cup_{i=1}^n \pa B(p_i,r)$. Since $\mathcal{S}_0 \cap \cup_{i=1}^n\pa B(p_i,r) = \emptyset$, there exist a $\rho>0$ such that $\mathcal{S}_0 \cap N_\rho(r)=\emptyset$. Then one follows the same argument as in Proposition \ref{prop: stability - general} applied to $r-\rho$.
   \end{remark}
As remarked above, a careful observation allows us to choose $r$ and $\delta$ in Proposition \ref{prop: stability - general} such that the boundary of $B(p,r)$ does not contain any critical points of $\varphi_t,\; |t|\leq \delta$. Moving forward, the choice of a sufficiently small $r$ will be made with this conditions in mind.



The following is an easy to prove (but fundamental) fact about $C^k$ convergence of eigenfunctions, $k \geq 1$ that was implicitly used in the proof of Proposition \ref{prop: stability - general}.
\begin{Lemma}\label{lem:lim_M_1}
Let $\NNN_t$ (respectively $\mathcal{C}_t$) and $\NNN_0$ (respectively $\mathcal{C}_0$) denote the nodal sets (respectively, critical sets)  corresponding to %^{th}$ eigenfunctions of $M_t$ and $M_1$ 
$\varphi_t$ and $\varphi_0$ respectively. Consider a sequence of points $x_i$ such that for each $i$, $x_i\in \NNN_t$ (respectively,  $\mathcal{C}_t$). For any limit point $x$ of $x_i$, we have $x\in \NNN_0$ (respectively, $\mathcal{C}_0$).
\end{Lemma}
For a proof in a more general setting, see \cite[Lemma 3.5]{MukherjeeSaha2022}. This has the following implication:
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:nodal_line_no_crossing}
     If $\varphi_0$ has no nodal critical point, then $\nu(\varphi_t) = \nu(\varphi_0)$ for sufficiently small $t$. %$%then $\varphi_t$ has the same number of nodal domains, for small enough $t$.  
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
    Since the nodal set $\NNN(\varphi_0)$ does not contain any nodal critical points, $\NNN(\varphi_0)$ is a union of disjoint embedded circles. Let $C_1, \cdots, C_p$ be the connected components ($1$-dimensional submanifolds) of the nodal set $\NNN(\varphi_0)$. We would like to show that for sufficiently small $t$, $\NNN(\varphi_t)$ contains exactly $p$ disjoint connected components which are respectively arbitrarily close to the $C_j$ respectively. 

    We begin by proving that given $\delta >$,  $\NNN(\varphi_t)$ is contained in a $\delta$-neighbourhood $U$ of $\NNN(\varphi_0)$ for small enough $|t|$. If possible, let there exist a sequence $\{t_n\}$ such that $\NNN(\varphi_{t_n})$ is not contained inside $U$.  By precompactness in Hausdorff metric (see, for example \cite[Theorem 2.2.25]{HenrotPierre2018}), one can extract a subsequence which converges to a set $X \not\subset U$, a contradiction, since by Lemma \ref{lem:lim_M_1} we already know that $X \subseteq \NNN(\varphi_0)$. Moreover, we can show that each $\delta$-neighbourhood $U_i$ of $C_i$ contains at least one component of $\NNN(\varphi_t)$. %If possible, let there exist a sequence $\{t_n\}\to 0$ such that $\NNN(\varphi_{t_n})$ contains no  components in $U_i$. In other words, $\varphi_{t_n}$ is non sign-changing on $U_i$. This assumption leads to a contradiction 
   This follows from the fact that $\varphi_t$ converges to $\varphi_0$ in $C^0(M)$ and $\varphi_0$ changes sign on each $U_i$, which in turn implies that $\varphi_{t_n}$ must change sign on each $U_i$.

    Finally, we prove that any $\delta$-neighbourhood of each $C_i$ contains at most one component of the nodal set of $\NNN(\varphi_t)$ for sufficiently small $t$. If possible, let there exist a sequence $t_n\to 0$ such that $\NNN(\varphi_{t_n})$ contains at least two components ($1-$dimensional submanifolds) of $\NNN(\varphi_{t_n})$ in the $\delta$-neighbourhood $U_1$ of $C_1$ (without loss of generality). Then we have two possibilities:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(A)]: The components of $\NNN(\varphi_t)$ intersects each other.
        \item[(B)]: $\NNN(\varphi_t)$ is a disjoint collection of embedded circles.
    \end{enumerate}
    By the eigenfunction convergence $\varphi_t \to \varphi_0$, it is clear that small perturbation cannot produce any nodal critical points. In other words, $\Cal{S}_t = \emptyset$ for small enough $t$, i.e., only Case (B) is possible.  In that case, there exists a nodal domain of $\varphi_{t_n}$ completely contained inside $U$ which contradicts the inner radius estimates for $\varphi_{t_n}$ if $\delta$ is chosen to be small enough. 
    \end{proof}

\section{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:loc_nod_est}}\label{Sec: Perturbation and critical points}

We divide this section into three subsections containing the proof of each statement in Theorem \ref{thm:loc_nod_est}. Before beginning with the proof, we introduce the following crucial lemma which shall be used in multiple instances throughout the remainder of the article. 

\begin{Lemma}\label{lem:nod_lin_disc_invariant}
    Let $p$ be an isolated nodal critical point of $\varphi_0$ with order of vanishing $k$. Consider an open ball $B(p,r)$ such that $\overline{B(p,r)}$ contains no other  nodal  critical point of $\varphi_0$. Then, the number of nodal lines of $\varphi_t$ emanating from $\pa B(p,r)$ remains invariant under small perturbation. %More precisely, within a small neighbourhood $T_{r,\rho}^-$ of $\pa B(p,r)$ $\displaystyle T_{r,\rho}^-:=\bigcup_{i=1}^n B(p_i,\tilde{r}: r-\rho\leq \tilde{r}\leq r\}$
\end{Lemma}

\begin{proof}
   %\color{blue} 
   We choose $r,\rho$ and $\delta$ as in Remark \ref{remark: mod_prop_2.6}. In other words, given $r,\rho$, there exist a $\delta:=\delta(r,\rho)$ such that
   \begin{align*}
       &S_t \cap \overline{B(p,r) \setminus B(p,r-\rho)}=\emptyset, \hspace{5mm} \forall |t| <\delta.
       %\iff & S_t \subset \cup_{i=1}^n B(p_i,r-\rho), \hspace{5mm} \forall |t| <\delta.
       \end{align*}
   \color{black}
   Therefore we can choose an open neighbourhood $U$ of $p$ such that %{\color{blue}
   $\pa U \subset B(p, r)\setminus \pa B(p,r-\rho$), $U$ is diffeomorphic to a disc, $\pa U$ contains no nodal critical point of $\varphi_0$ and $\nabla \varphi_0$ is not perpendicular to $\pa U$.  
   Consider a smooth regular parametrisation $\eta: \SSS^1 \to \pa U$. % of the boundary of the disc centred at a nodal critical point $p$ of $\varphi_0$. 
   Then $\psi_t:=\varphi_t \circ \tilde{\eta}: \RR \to \RR$ is a family of $2\pi$ periodic functions, each having simple zeros, where $\tilde{\eta} : \RR \to \pa U$ is the lift of $\eta$. Given the condition on the boundary of the neighbourhood $U$, we have $\psi'_t \neq 0$ on $\RR$. Then the number of zeros $n_t$ of $\varphi_t$ on $\pa U$ is given by a winding number characterisation (for example, see \cite[Lemma 1.1]{HungerbuhlerWasem2018}):
   \begin{equation*}
       n_t = \frac{1}{\pi}\int_0^{2\pi} \frac{\psi_t'(x)^2-\psi_t(x)\psi_t''(x)}{\psi_t(x)^2+\psi_t'(x)^2}\;dx.
   \end{equation*}
By dominated convergence theorem we see that $t \to n_t$ is a continuous integer valued function on $[-\delta,\delta]$. Hence $n_t=n_0 \quad \forall |t|<\delta$.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}\label{Remark: 3.2.}
    Without loss of generality, we can assume that $U=B(p,\tilde{r})$ for some $r-\rho\leq\tilde{r}\leq r$. Therefore, the number of nodal lines emanating from $\pa B(p, \tilde{r})$ is invariant for all $|t|<\delta$ and $r-\rho \leq \tilde{r} \leq r$.
\end{remark}


Before we proceed further, we impose a graph structure on the nodal set $\NNN(\varphi_t)$ restricted to $B(p,r)$. We denote the nodal graph by $G_t:=\NNN(\varphi_t) \cap B(p,r)$. The set of vertices $V_t$ consists of nodal critical points of $\varphi_t$ in $B(p,r)$ i.e. $V_t:= \mathcal{S}_t \cap B(p,r)$. Looking at $\NNN(\varphi_t) \cap B(p,r)$, the nodal set can be viewed as edges and ``half-edges'' of the graph $G_t$. Here, the edges are nodal line segments connecting two nodal critical points of $\varphi_t$ inside $B(p,r)$, and half-edges are nodal line segments with a nodal critical point as one endpoint and the other endpoint on $\pa B(p,r)$ (as shown in Figure \ref{fig:nodal_graph_ex}). By Lemma \ref{lem:nod_lin_disc_invariant}, there are precisely $2k$ half edges of $G_t$ for all small enough $t$. %, {\color{blue} where $k$ being the vanishing order of $\varphi_0$ at $p$}. 
From our previous discussions, note that $G_t$ is a tree (possibly disconnected) when $r$ is small enough, i.e., $G_t$ does not contain a cycle. 

