\subsection{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:loc_nod_est}(a)}
We want to prove that given $r$ small enough, there exists $\varepsilon := \varepsilon(r) > 0$ such that for $|t| < \varepsilon$ the number of nodal critical points of $\varphi_t$ inside $B(p, r)$ does not exceed $k-1$,  where $k$ is the order of vanishing of $\varphi_0$ at $p\in M$.

If possible, let there be $m >k-1$ nodal critical points of $\varphi_t$ in $B(p,r)$. Observe that the nodal critical points of any eigenfunction $\varphi$ have an order of vanishing at least two, that is, at least four nodal line segments emanate from each nodal critical point.  %Observe that each of the nodal critical points of $\varphi_t$ has an order of vanishing at least two, i.e. at least four nodal line segments emanate from each nodal critical point. 
Let ${C_t}$ denote the number of connected components of the nodal graph $G_t=\NNN(\varphi_t) \cap B(p,r)$ and $\{G_t^1,G_t^2,\cdots,G_t^{C_t}\}$ be the set of maximal connected subgraphs of $G_t$. Moreover, for each $i\in \{1, \cdots, C_t\}$, assume that $m_i$ is the number of nodal critical points (i.e. vertices) in each $G_t^i$ such that $\sum_{i=1}^{C_t} m_i = m$ and $\tilde{k}_{i,l} \; (l=1,\cdots,m_i)$ be the order of vanishing of the vertices in $G_t^i$. For reference, in Figure \ref{fig:nodal_graph_ex}, $C_t=2, m_1=3, m_2=3$, and $\tilde{k}_{i,1}=\tilde{k}_{i,3}=2; \tilde{k}_{i,2}=1~(i=1,2)$. 


We note that each component $G_t^i$ is connected to the boundary $\pa B(p,r)$ by $2b_i$ (say) number of half-edges and  from Lemma \ref{lem:nod_lin_disc_invariant} we have that $\sum_{i=1}^{C_t} 2b_i = 2k$. % We have  and where the latter equality follows. 
Therefore $G_i$ has exactly $\sum_{l=1}^{m_i} \tilde{k}_{i,l} - b_i$ edges connecting $m_i$ vertices and rest $2b_i$ half-edges connect $G_t^i$ to $\pa B(p,r)$. Since any tree with $n$ vertices can have at most $n-1$ edges, we impose the restrictions $\sum_{l=1}^{m_i} \tilde{k}_{i,l}-b_i \leq m_i-1$ for all $i=1, \cdots, C_t$. Summing over $i$, we have
\begin{align}
    &\left(\sum_{i=1}^{C_t}\sum_{l=1}^{m_i} \tilde{k}_{i,l}\right)-k \leq m-{C_t} 
     \iff \left(\sum_{i=1}^{C_t}\sum_{l=1}^{m_i} \tilde{k}_{i,l} \right)-m \leq k-{C_t}. \nonumber
\end{align}
The above inequality can be rewritten in terms of index as
\begin{equation}\label{eqn: index_connected_component_relation}
   \sum_{i=1}^{C_t}\sum_{l=1}^{m_i} (\tilde{k}_{i,l}-1) = \sum_{i=1}^{C_t}\sum_{q \in G_t^i} |\ind_q(\nabla \varphi_t)|=  \sum_{q \in \mathcal{S}_t \cap B(p,r)} |\ind_q(\nabla \varphi_t)|  
   \leq k-{C_t}.
\end{equation}
Since $|\ind_q(\nabla \varphi_t)| \geq 1$ for any $q \in \mathcal{S}_t$ and $C_t \geq 1$, we have $m\leq k-1$, a contradiction. \qed


\subsection{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:loc_nod_est}(b)} 
    Our goal is to prove that given $r$ small enough, there exists $\varepsilon := \varepsilon(r) > 0$ such that for $|t| < \varepsilon$, %there are only finitely many \SSa{nodal?} critical points of $\varphi_t$ within $B(p, r)$, and 
    the order of vanishing of any nodal critical point of $\varphi_t$ within $B(p, r)$ cannot be greater than $k$. 

    If possible, let $\varphi_t$ has a critical point $q$ with order of vanishing $l>k$. From \cite{Cheng1976}, we know that $\NNN(\varphi_t)$ consists of $2l$ line segments emanating from $q$ and they form an equiangular system at $q$. 
     Therefore, we have $l$ nodal lines intersecting at $q$. The continuation of each nodal line on both ends either reach the boundary $\pa B(p,r)$ or merges to create a closed curve. Let $e_1, \cdots, e_{l}$ denote the continuations of these nodal curves  inside $B(p,r)$. Each of these continued nodal lines $e_i$, when restricted to $\overline{B(p, r)}$, is either a curve that intersects $\pa B(p,r)$ at exactly two points or forms a closed curve inside $\overline{B(p,r)}$.


    Due to Lemma \ref{lem:nod_lin_disc_invariant},  the number of nodal segments $e_i$ whose both ends reach $\pa B(p,r)$ is exactly $k$. Therefore, there exists one nodal segment, say $e_{k+1}$, which is a closed curve inside $\overline{B(p,r)}$. This implies that a nodal domain of $\varphi_t$ is contained inside $B(p,r)$. This contradicts the Faber-Krahn inequality (see \cite{GeorgievMukherjee2018,CharronMangoubi2024}), given that $r$ is sufficiently small. \qed




Before beginning with the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:loc_nod_est}(c), we introduce some notations and definitions for ease of explanation. Consider some fixed $K\in \NN$ and a ball $B(p, r)$. % centred at $p$ with radius $r$. 
Define a collection 
$$\mathcal{L}_K:=\left\{L=\bigcup_{j=1}^K l_j : \text{each } l_j \text{ is a 1-dimensional submanifold of $M$ which satisfies (1)-(3) below}\right\}:$$
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $l_j \subset \overline{B(p,r)}$ and $l_j$ connects two points on $\pa B(p,r)$ for all $j=\{1, \cdots, K\}$. Note that self-intersections are \underline{\em not} allowed.
    \item ($\cup_{j=1}^K l_j) \cap \pa B(p, r)$ divides $\pa B(p, r)$ into $2K$ arcs.
    \item For any $L\in \mathcal{L}_K$, every connected component of $B(p, r)\setminus L$ shares a non-zero capacity boundary with $\pa B(p, r)$. We call each of these connected components of $B(p, r)\setminus L$ as a {\em sector} corresponding to $L$.
\end{enumerate}
%\SSa{@MM; can we rephrase (3) in a better way? I don't like the way it's written.}


Note that $L$ can have multiple connected components. Also, (2) and (3) exclude the following kinds of configurations of $L$ in $\mathcal{L}_K$. 
