\subsection{Pair Hopf algebroid and classical jet Hopf algebroid $\CJ(B)$}\label{secpair}

Given an algebra $B$, there is a well-known {\em pair Hopf algebroid} $B\ot \BB$, with  the $B^e$-ring structure
\[s(a)=a\ot 1,\quad t(a)=1\ot a,\quad (a\ot a')(b\ot b')=aa'\ot b'b,\]
for any $a,a',b,b'\in B$.
And the $B$-coring structure
\[\Delta(a\ot a')=a\ot 1\diamond_{B}1\ot a',\quad \varepsilon(a\ot a')=aa'.\]
It is not hard to see $B\ot \BB$ is in fact a Hopf algebroid with
\[(a\ot a')_{+}\ot_{\overline{B}}(a\ot a')_{-}=a\ot 1\ot_{\overline{B}} a'\ot 1,\quad (a\ot a')_{[+]}\ot_{B}(a\ot a')_{[-]}=1\ot a'\ot_{B} 1\ot a.\]
 There is a left ideal of $B\ot \BB$, defined by
\[\mu_k:=\{(a\ot b)(\extd_{\textup{uni}}a_{0})\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}a_{1})\cdots \,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}a_{k})|\forall a,b,a_0, a_1,\cdots a_k\in B\}=(\Omega^1_{\rm uni})^{k+1},\]
where $\extd_{\textup{uni}}a=1\ot a-a\ot 1\in B\ot \BB$ and $\mu=\mu_0=\Omega^1_{\rm uni}=\ker(m_{B}:B
\tens B\to B)$ is the `universal calculus' on $B$. We can see $\mu_n\subseteq\cdots \subseteq\mu_1\subseteq \mu_0$. Following \cite{Ver}, we let
\[ \CJ^{k}(B):=B^{e}/\mu_k\]
 be the $k$-th Jet bundle over $B$. We can see $\CJ^{k}(B)$ has a canonical left $B^e$-module structure.  If $B$ is commutative, $\mu_{k}$ is a 2-side ideal. Hence we have a sequence
\[ B\twoheadleftarrow \CJ^1(B) \twoheadleftarrow \CJ^2(B) \twoheadleftarrow \CJ^3(B) \twoheadleftarrow\cdots\]
and take the projective limit $\CJ^\infty(B)$. We proceed informally and write $\CJ^\infty(B)=B^{e}/\mu_{\infty}$.



\begin{lemma}\label{lem. classical jet Hopf algebroid}
   If $B$ is a commutative algebra such that $\mu_\infty$ exists then $\CJ^\infty(B)$ is a Hopf algebroid as quotient of the pair Hopf algebroid $B^e$. We call this Hopf algebroid jet Hopf algebroid and denote it by $\CJ(B)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We give an informal proof. By Proposition \ref{prop. quotient Hopf algebroids}, it is sufficient to show $\mu_\infty$ is a Hopf ideal. We first show $\mu_\infty$ is a coideal of $B^{e}$. Indeed,
\begin{align*}
    \Delta(\extd_{\textup{uni}}a)=&1\ot1\diamond_{B}1\ot a-a\ot1\diamond_{B}1\ot 1\\
    =&1\ot1\diamond_{B}1\ot a-1\ot1\diamond_{B}a\ot 1+1\ot a\diamond_{B}1\ot 1-a\ot1\diamond_{B}1\ot 1\\
    =&1\ot1\diamond_{B}\extd_{\textup{uni}}a+\extd_{\textup{uni}}a\diamond_{B}1\ot 1.
\end{align*}
Therefore, $\mu_{\infty}$ is a coideal since the $\Delta$ is an algebra map and $\mu_\infty$ belongs to the kernal of $\varepsilon$.
    Next, it is sufficient to show that $\mu_\infty$ is a Hopf ideal. Indeed,
    \begin{align*}
        (\extd_{\textup{uni}}a)_{+}&\ot_{\overline{B}}(\extd_{\textup{uni}}a)_{-}\\
        =&1\ot1\ot_{\overline{B}}a\ot 1-a\ot1\ot_{\overline{B}}1\ot 1\\
        =&1\ot1\ot_{\overline{B}}a\ot 1-1\ot1\ot_{\overline{B}}1\ot a+1\ot a\ot_{\overline{B}}1\ot 1-a\ot1\ot_{\overline{B}}1\ot 1\\
        =&-1\ot1\ot_{\overline{B}}\extd_{\textup{uni}}a+\extd_{\textup{uni}}a\ot_{\overline{B}}1\ot 1.
    \end{align*}
By using $(\extd_{\textup{uni}}a\, \extd_{\textup{uni}}b)_{+}\ot (\extd_{\textup{uni}}a\, \extd_{\textup{uni}}b)_{-}=(\extd_{\textup{uni}}a)_{+}\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b)_{+}\ot (\extd_{\textup{uni}}b)_{-}\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}a)_{-}$, we have the result.    Similarly, for the anti-left Hopf ideal condition.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark} \label{remJ1}
We also note that for each $k$ we have
\[ 0\leftarrow \CJ^{k-1}(B) \leftarrow \CJ^k(B) \leftarrow \Omega_S^k(B)\leftarrow 0\]
as a short exact sequence, where $\Omega_S^k(B)$ is defined \cite{MaSim} as the joint kernel of all adjacent wedge products on the tensor algebra $T_B(\Omega^1(B))$. Here  $\Omega^1(B)= \mu/\mu^2$ is the space of 1-forms in classical (algebraic) geometry, so this is clear for $k=1$.  However, any sub-bimodule of $\mu=\Omega^1_{\rm uni}$ defines a space of 1-forms (or first order differential calculus)   so we are at liberty to introduce a larger differential calculus
\[ \Omega_k^1(B):={\mu/ \mu^{k+1}}\]
so that
\begin{equation}\label{JkOmegak} 0\leftarrow B \leftarrow \CJ^k(B) \leftarrow \Omega^1_k(B)\leftarrow 0\end{equation}
 is a short exact sequence and the jet prolongation map $j_k:B\to\CJ^k(B)$ can be formulated in terms of this. Here $\pi: \CJ^k(B)\to \Omega^1_k(B)$, $\pi(a\tens b)=(\extd a)b$ splits the inclusion of $\Omega_k^1(B)$ giving a projection so that $\CJ^k(B)=B\oplus \Omega^1_k(B)$ where $B=[1\tens B]$ viewed in $\CJ^k(B)$. We then define
 \[ j_k(b)=b\oplus \extd b= [1\tens b+ b\tens 1-1\tens b]=[b\tens 1]\]
  as the jet prolongation. This is a left module map and hence defined by $j_k(1)=[1\tens 1]$ mod $\CN$.  In other words, we can use a non-standard differential structure on $A$ to encode the higher order jet bundles and prolongation maps as if 1-jets but for a different calculus.

In the case of an algebraic group, we can translate everything to the identity and $\mu\cong B\tens B^+$ where $B^+$ is the kernel of the counit. Then $\mu^{k+1}\cong B\tens (B^+)^{k+1}$ and $\Omega^1_k(B)\cong B \tens B^+/(B^+)^{k+1}$. For example, for $B=\C[x]$, $B\tens B=\C[x,y]$, $\mu\cong \C[x]\tens \<y\>$ and $\Omega^1_k(B)=\C[x]\tens \<y\>/\<y^{k+1}\>$ is a $k$-dimensional calculus where the exterior derivative contains the $k$-fold usual derivatives.
\end{remark}

If $B$ is not commutative, Lemma~\ref{lem. classical jet Hopf algebroid} is no more correct as in general $(\extd_{\textup{uni}}a)(b\ot c)$ doesn't belong to the kernel of $\varepsilon$, so in general we can't define a Hopf ideal. Even so, any first order differential calculus $\Omega^1(B)$ is given by $\mu/\CN$ for some sub-bimodule $\CN\subseteq\mu$ and we can {\em define} the associated jet bundle and jet prolongation map as
\[  \CJ^1(B) :=(B\tens B)/\CN,\quad j_1(a)=[a\tens 1].\]
As a left $B$-module, we can think of $\CJ= B^e/I$ for $I$ a left ideal of $A^e$. This is equivalent to $\CJ^1(B)=B\oplus\Omega^1(B)$ in \cite{MaSim,Flo}. This is a general setting for noncommutative geometry (it is the higher $\CJ^k(B)$ that are less clear) and the above remark says that this more general $\CJ^1(B)$ for $\CN=\mu^k$ or calculus $\Omega^1_k$ reduces when $B$ is commutative to the usual $\CJ^k(B)$.


\subsection{Dual pairing with Hopf algebroid of differential operators of algebras}\label{secpairdif}

It is explained in the introduction, results in \cite{Ver} imply that given a smooth manifold $M$, the $k$-th order differential operators is isomorphic to the dual $k$-th jet bundle $\CJ^{k}(B)$, where $B=C^{\infty}(M)$. We can generalise this idea to any noncommutative algebra as follows. More precisely,
given an algebra $B$, and any $b\in B$, we define $\delta_{b}:\Hom(B, B)\to \Hom(B, B)$ by
\[\delta_{b}(D)(a)=D(a)b-D(ab),\]
for any $a\in B$ and $D\in \Hom(B, B)$. We define the $k$-th order differential operators of $B$
\[\textup{Diff}^{\textup{k}}(B)=\{D\in \Hom(B,B)\,|\,\delta_{b_{0}}\circ\delta_{b_{1}}\cdots \circ\delta_{b_{k}}(D)=0, \forall b_{0},\cdots b_{k}\in B\}.\]
It is similar to the classical case in \cite{Ver}, we have
\begin{lemma}\label{lem. vbski}
    Let $B$ be an algebra (not necessary commutative). Then $\textup{Diff}^{\textup{k}}(B)\cong \Hom_{\BB-}(\CJ^{k}(B),B)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $D\in \textup{Diff}^{\textup{k}}(B)$, we can define
\[\phi_{D}([a\ot b])=D(a)b.\]
Clearly, $\phi_{D}$ is left $\BB$-linear.
Also, we can see $\phi_{D}$ factors through $\mu_k$ by the definition of the $k$-th order differential operator. Indeed, we can show the following inductively
\begin{align*}
    \phi_{D}([(b\ot b')(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{1})\cdots \,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{k})])=\delta_{b_{0}}\circ\delta_{b_{1}}\cdots \circ\delta_{b_{k}}(D)(b)\,b'=0.
\end{align*}
For $k=0$,
\begin{align*}
   \phi_{D}([(b\ot b')(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})])=\phi_{D}([(b\ot b')(1\ot b_{0}-b_{0}\ot 1)])\\
   =D(b)b_{0}\,b'-D(b\,b_{0})b'=\delta_{b_{0}}(D)(b)b'.
\end{align*}
Assume this is true for $k=n$, we can see
\begin{align*}
     \phi_{D}&([(b\ot b')(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n+1})\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n})\cdots \,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})])\\
     =&\phi_{D}([(b\ot b')(1\ot b_{n+1})(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n})\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n-1})\cdots \,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})])\\
     &-\phi_{D}([(b\ot b')(b_{n+1}\ot 1)(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n})\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n-1})\cdots \,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})])\\
     =&\delta_{b_{n}}\circ\delta_{b_{n-1}}\cdots \circ\delta_{b_{0}}(D)(b)b_{n+1}\,b'-\delta_{b_{n}}\circ\delta_{b_{n-1}}\cdots \circ\delta_{b_{0}}(D)(b\,b_{n+1})\,b'\\
     =&\delta_{b_{n+1}}\circ\delta_{b_{n}}\cdots \circ\delta_{b_{0}}(D)(b)\,b'.
\end{align*}
% where the 2nd step uses the fact that $\phi_{D}([(b\ot b')(1\ot a)])=\phi_{D}([b\ot a
% \, b'])$ and $\phi_{D}([(b\ot b')(a\ot 1)])=\phi_{D}([b\,a\ot
% , b'])$.

Conversely, let $\phi\in  \Hom_{\BB-}(\CJ^{k}(B),B)$, we can define a  $k$-th order differential operator $D_{\phi}$ by
\[D_{\phi}(a)=\phi([a\ot 1]).\]
By a similar inductive method, we can see
\begin{align*}
    \delta_{b_{0}}\circ\delta_{b_{1}}\cdots \circ\delta_{b_{k}}(D_{\phi})(b)=\phi([(b\ot 1)(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{1})\cdots \,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{k})])=0.
\end{align*}
Indeed, let $k=0$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \delta_{b_{0}}(D_{\phi})(b)=D_{\phi}(b)b_{0}-D_{\phi}(b\,b_{0})=\phi([(b\ot 1)(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})]).
\end{align*}
Assume this is true for $k=n$ and $\phi\in \Hom_{\BB-}(\CJ^{n}(B),B)$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \delta_{b_{n+1}}&\circ\delta_{b_{n}}\cdots \circ\delta_{b_{0}}(D_{\phi})(b)\\
    =&\delta_{b_{n}}\circ\delta_{b_{n-1}}\cdots \circ\delta_{b_{0}}(D_{\phi})(b)b_{n+1}-\delta_{b_{n}}\circ\delta_{b_{n-1}}\cdots \circ\delta_{b_{0}}(D_{\phi})(b\,b_{n+1})\\
    =&\phi([(b\ot 1)(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n})\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n-1})\cdots \,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})])b_{n+1}\\
    &-\phi([(b\,b_{n+1}\ot 1)(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n})\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n-1})\cdots \,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})])\\
    =&\phi([(b\ot b_{n+1})(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n})\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n-1})\cdots \,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})])\\
    &-\phi([(b\,b_{n+1}\ot 1)(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n})\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n-1})\cdots \,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})])\\
    =&\phi([(b\ot 1)(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n+1})\,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{n})\cdots \,(\extd_{\textup{uni}}b_{0})]),
\end{align*}
where the 3rd step uses the fact that $\phi$ is left $\BB$-linear.
Moreover,
\begin{align*}
    D_{\phi_{D}}(a)=\phi_{D}([a\ot 1])=D(a),
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
    \phi_{D_{\phi}}([a\ot b])=D_{\phi}(a)b=\phi([a\ot 1])b=\phi([a\ot b]).
\end{align*}
\end{proof}



It is given by \cite{Xu}, let $B$ be a commutative algebra (which is always viewed as the smooth functions $C^\infty(M)$ of a smooth manifold $M$ in \cite{Xu}), the algebra of all differential operators $\cD(B)$ is a left bialgebroid over $B$. More precisely, the source and target maps are
\[s(a)(b)=ab,\qquad t(a)(b)=ba, \qquad\forall a,b\in B.\]
The product is operator composition. In addition, the coproduct and counit are given by
\[\Delta(D)(a\ot b)=\Delta(ab),\qquad \varepsilon(D)=D(1).\]









\begin{theorem}
    Let $B$ be a commutative algebra such that the limit $\mu_\infty$ exists. Then there is a dual pairing between  $\cD(B)$ and $\CJ(B)$. More precisely, the dual pairing is
    \begin{align*}
        \<D|[a\ot b]\>=D(a)b,
    \end{align*}
    for any $[a\ot b]\in \CJ(B)$ and $D\in \cD(B)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    The dual pairing is well defined by Lemma \ref{lem. vbski} as any different operator factors through $\mu_\infty$. First, we observe that
    \begin{align*}
         \<c\overline{d}\,D\,e\overline{f}|[a\ot b]\>g=(c\circ\overline{d}\circ D\circ e \circ\overline{f})(a)\,b\,g=c\,D(eaf)\,d\,b\,g=c\,\<D|e\,\overline{g}[a\ot b]\,f\,\overline{d}\>.
    \end{align*}
    % Here, we specially notice that $\<\overline{d}\,D|[a\ot b]\>=\overline{d}\circ D(a)\,b=D(a)\,d\,b$.
    Second, for any $[a\ot b],[c\ot d]\in \CJ(B)$ and $D\in \cD(B)$, we can see on the one hand
    \begin{align*}
        \<D|[a\ot b][c\ot d]\>=\<D|[ac\ot db]\>=D(ac)\,db.
    \end{align*}
    On the other hand
    \begin{align*}
        \<D\o|[a\ot b]\,\overline{\<D\t|[c\ot d]\>}\>=\<D\o|[a\ot D\t(c)db]\>=D\o(a)\, D\t(c)\,db=D(ac)\,db.
    \end{align*}
    Also, $\<D|[1\ot 1]\>=\varepsilon(D)$. Third, for any $[a\ot b]\in \CJ(B)$ and $D,D'\in \cD(B)$, we can see on the one hand,
    \begin{align*}
    \<D'\circ D|[a\ot b]\>= D'(D(a))\,b.
    \end{align*}
    On the other hand,
    \begin{align*}
        \<D'|\<D|[a\ot 1]\>[1\ot b]\>= \<D'|[D(a)\ot b]\>=D'(D(a))\,b.
    \end{align*}
    Also, $\<1|[a\ot b]\>=a\, b=\varepsilon([a\ot b])$.
\end{proof}



\subsection{Cotwist quantization of jet Hopf algebroids}\label{secquant}

Until now, we have constructed the $k$-th jet bundle $\CJ^{k}(B)$ and the $k$-th differential operators for any algebra $B$. However, for the jet Hopf algebroid $\CJ(B)$ we needed $B$ to be commutative (and then so is $\CJ(B)$). On the other hand, it is shown in \cite{Xu} that given a smooth manifold $M$, there is a deformed algebra structure on $C^{\infty}(M)$ with a new product $\ast:C^{\infty}(M)\ot C^{\infty}(M)\to C^{\infty}(M)$. More precisely, the new product is induced by an invertible left 2-cocycle $F$ in the bialgebroid of differential operators $\cD(C^{\infty}(M))$ in the sense that
\[a\ast\,b=a\cdot_{F}b=\varepsilon(F^{\alpha}\,a)\varepsilon(F_{\alpha}\,b)=(F^{\alpha}\circ\,a)(1)(F_{\alpha}\circ\,b)(1)=F^{\alpha}(a)\,F_{\alpha}(b),\]
for any $a,b\in C^{\infty}(M)$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:quant}
    Let $M$ be a smooth manifold and $B=C^{\infty}(M)$. If $\mu_\infty$ exists and $F$ is an invertible left 2-cocycle in $\cD(B)$ inducing an deformed product as above then there is an invertible 2-cocycle $\Gamma$ on $\CJ(B)$ which is given by
\[\Gamma([a\ot b], [c\ot d])=(a\ast c)\,db,\]
with inverse
\[\Gamma^{-1}([a\ot b], [c\ot d])=(ac)\ast(d\ast b).\]
Moreover, the left $\BG$-Hopf algebroid structure on $\CJ(B)^{\Gamma}$ is
\[s(b)=[b\ot 1],\quad t(b)=[1\ot b],\quad[a\ot b]\CG [c\ot d]=[a\ast c\ot d\ast b],\]
and
\[\Delta^{\Gamma}([a\ot b])=[a\ot 1]\diamond_{\BG}[1\ot b],\qquad \varepsilon^{\Gamma}([a\ot b])=a\ast b,\]
and
\[[a\ot b]_{\p}\ot [a\ot b]_{\m}=[a\ot 1]\ot [b\ot 1].\]
In addition, the twisted dual pairing between the twisted differential operators and the jet Hopf algebroid is
\[\<D|[a\ot b]\>^{\Gamma}=D(a)\ast b.\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    As $F$ is an invertible left 2-cocycle in $\cD(B)$, by Lemma \ref{lem. 2-cocycle dual}, we can construct an invertible left 2-cocycle $\Gamma$ by $\Gamma(X\ot Y)=\<F^{\alpha}|X\overline{\<F_{\alpha}|Y\>}\>$ for any $X, Y\in \CJ(B)$. More precisely,
    \begin{align*}
        \Gamma([a\ot b], [c\ot d])=&\<F^{\alpha}|[a\ot b]\overline{\<F_{\alpha}|[c\ot d]\>}\>=\<F^{\alpha}|[a\ot b]\overline{F_{\alpha}(c)d}\>\\
        =&\<F^{\alpha}|[a\ot F_{\alpha}(c)d\,b]\>=F^{\alpha}(a)F_{\alpha}(c)db\\
        =&(a\ast c)\,db.
    \end{align*}
It is given by Theorem \ref{thm. invertible 2 cocycle} that the inverse of $\Gamma$ is given by
\begin{align*}
    \Gamma^{-1}&([a\ot b], [c\ot d])\\
    =&\Gamma([a\ot b]_{+}\,[c\ot d]_{+},\Gamma([c\ot d]_{-}\o,\,[a\ot b]_{-}\o)[c\ot d]_{-}\t\,[a\ot b]_{-}\t)\\
    =&\Gamma([ac\ot 1],\Gamma([d\ot 1]\,,[b\ot 1])[1\ot 1])\\
    =&\Gamma([ac\ot 1],[d\ast\,b\ot 1])\\
    =&(ac)\ast (d\ast b).
\end{align*}
For the left Hopf algebroid structure, we can see firstly
\[a\CG b=\Gamma([a\ot 1], [b\ot 1])=a\ast b.\]
We can also see
\begin{align*}
    [a\ot& b]\CG[c\ot d]\\
    =&\Gamma([a\ot b]\o,\,[c\ot d]\o)[a\ot b]\t{}_{+}\,[c\ot d]\t{}_{+}\,\overline{\Gamma([c\ot d]\t{}_{-},\,[a\ot b]\t{}_{-})}\\
     =&\Gamma([a\ot 1],\,[c\ot 1])[1\ot 1]\,[1\ot 1]\,\overline{\Gamma([d\ot 1],\,[b\ot 1])}\\
     =&[a\ast c\ot d\ast b].
\end{align*}
To see the twisted coproduct is the one given above, it is sufficient to check
\begin{align*}
    \GH(&[a\ot 1]\diamond_{\BG}[1\ot b])\\
    =&[a\ot 1]_{+}\overline{\Gamma([a\ot 1]_{-}, [1\ot b]\o)}\diamond_{B}[1\ot b]\t\\
    =&[a\ot 1]\overline{\Gamma([1\ot 1], [1\ot 1])}\diamond_{B}[1\ot b]\\
    =&\Delta([a\ot b]).
\end{align*}
For the counit, we have
\begin{align*}
    \varepsilon^{\Gamma}([a\ot b])=\Gamma([a\ot b]_{+}, [a\ot b]_{-})=\Gamma([a\ot 1], [b\ot 1])=a\ast\,b.
\end{align*}
To see the twisted left Hopf structure is the one given above, it is sufficient to check
\begin{align*}
    \GH(&[a\ot 1]\ot [b\ot 1])\\
    =&[a\ot 1]_{+}\ot [b\ot 1]_{+}\overline{\Gamma([b\ot 1]_{-}, [a\ot 1]_{-})}\\
    =&[a\ot 1]\ot [b\ot 1]
    =[a\ot b]_{+}\ot [a\ot b]_{-}.
\end{align*}
By Theorem \ref{thm. twisted dual pairing}, we can compute that
\begin{align*}
    \<D|[a\ot b]\>^{\Gamma}=&\Gamma(\<D|[a\ot b]\o\> [a\ot b]\t{}_{+},[a\ot b]\t{}_{-})\\
    =&\Gamma(\<D|[a\ot 1]\> [1\ot 1],[b\ot 1])\\
    =&\Gamma([D(a)\ot 1], [b\ot 1])\\
    =&D(a)\ast\,b.
\end{align*}
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
    It seems that a twisted Jet Hopf algebroid can be given by a quotient of the Hopf algebroid $\BG\ot \overline{\BG}$ by a Hopf ideal, but in general we can't similarly define a Hopf ideal $\mu_{\infty}$ associated to a noncommutative algebra $\BG$. Moreover, if we generate a 2-sides ideal of $\BG\ot \overline{\BG}$, then it is not hard to see that such an ideal can't be factored through by differential operators. For example, let $D$ be a $0$-th differential operator on $B$, we have $D([(\extd_{\textup{uni}}a)(b\ot c)])=D([b\ot c\,a-ab\ot c])=D(b)ca-D(ab)c\neq 0$. Therefore, we can't directly construct a noncommutative Jet Hopf algebroid by something like $\BG\ot \overline{\BG}/\mu_{\infty}(B^{\Gamma})$.
\end{remark}


