		Note that if $x_1,\ldots,x_K$ are continuous (or weakly differentiable) and if the boundary values coincide (i.e., $x_k(t_{k+1})=x_{k+1}(t_{k+1})$ for all $k\in \{0,\ldots,K-1\}$), then the concatenation is also continuous (or weakly differentiable). \blue{The analogous} claim also holds true for the adjoint state.

