\section{Simulation Results}\label{sec:simulation}

In this section, we examine the numerical performance of the proposed methods in Section \ref{sec:CROMS} and Section \ref{sec:CROiMS}, respectively. All the simulation results are based on 100 independent replications. For each replication, an independent draw of a labeled dataset of size $n$ and a test dataset of size $m$ is generated. %generated from the same distribution of labeled data. 
For simplicity, we denote the test dataset as $\gD_{\rm test} = \{(X_{j},Y_{j})\}_{j=n+1}^{n+m}$, and let $\widehat{\gU}(X_{j})$ and $\hat{z}(X_{j})$ be the prediction set and decision returned by each method for test point $X_{j}$. 
%Additional details of the simulation setting are provided in Appendix \ref{appen:simulation_setting}.

\subsection{Model selection in the averaged case} \label{Simulation: GCP}

In this subsection, we compare our methods \texttt{F-CROMS}, \texttt{E-CROMS} with two baseline methods:
\begin{itemize}
     \item \texttt{Naive-CP}: The model $S_{\lambda}$ used in the CRO procedure is randomly selected from all pre-trained models $\{S_{\lambda}:\lambda \in \Lambda\}$, and then the conformal prediction set (\ref{eq:conformal_set_single_model}) is constructed for the randomly selected model.

     \item \texttt{E2E}: The labeled dataset $\{(X_i,Y_i)\}_{i=1}^n$ is split into two parts, $\gD_1$ and $\gD_2$, where $\gD_1$ is used to select a model via \texttt{E-CROMS}, and $\gD_2$ is used to construct the conformal prediction set (\ref{eq:conformal_set_single_model} for the selected model. For annotations such as ``\texttt{E2E-0.75}'', it means that the dataset is split such that  $|\gD_1| = 0.75n$, $|\gD_2|=0.25n$. The detailed procedure of \texttt{E2E} is outlined in Appendix \ref{appen:split_CROMS}, which is adapted from the end-to-end approach in \citet{yeh2024end}. 
\end{itemize}
For comparison, we evaluate the following metrics on the test dataset for each method.
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] \emph{Marginal miscoverage}: $\frac{1}{m}\sum_{j=n+1}^{n+m} \mathbbm{1}\left\{Y_{j} \notin \widehat{\mathcal{U}}(X_{j})\right\}$;

    \item[(ii)] \emph{Marginal misrobustness}: $\frac{1}{m}\sum_{j=n+1}^{n+m} \mathbbm{1}\left\{\phi(Y_{j}, \hat{z}(X_{j})) > \max_{c\in \widehat{\gU}(X_{j})}\phi(c,\hat{z}(X_{j}))\right\}$.

       \item[(iii)] \emph{Average loss}: $\frac{1}{m} \sum_{j = n+1}^{n+m} \phi(Y_{j},\hat{z}(X_{j}))$;
\end{itemize}
Both marginal miscoverage and marginal misrobustness are expected to be less than or equal to the nominal level $\alpha$. Average loss is used to assess decision efficiency, with lower values indicating higher efficiency. The omitted settings and definitions of this simulation are deferred to Appendix \ref{appen:setting_simulation_GCP}.

\textbf{CRO setup under classification task.} We present simulation results for a classification task with $\mathcal{Y} =\{1,2,3, 4,5\}$ and $\mathcal{Z} = \{1,2,3,4,5\}$. The loss function is defined as $\phi(y,z) = M_{y,z}$ for $z\in \gZ$ and $y\in \gY$.
The loss function carries practical clinical meaning in our framework as discussed in the real application of Section \ref{sec:application}: $\mathcal{Y}$ represents ordinal disease severity levels, and $\mathcal{Z}$ corresponds to available treatment options.
The loss structure encodes two key clinical principles: (1) zero loss when the treatment perfectly matches disease severity ($\phi(1,1) = 0$), and (2) high loss for severe patients ($y=5$) when the treatment mismatched. %Second, and more critically, for patients with severe conditions ($y=5$), all treatment options $z \in \mathcal{Z}/\{5\}$ result in substantial losses due to delayed intervention. 
This explicitly captures the dual dependence of clinical costs on both disease severity and treatment appropriateness, where costs increase with disease progression and the growth of a mismatch between the condition and treatment.


The labeled data and test data are i.i.d. generated by
$
p(Y=k|X) \propto \exp(-v_k(X)) \text{ for } k\in \gY,
$
where the covariate $X = (X_1,...,X_7) \in \mathbb{R}^{7}$. The first four variables $X_i,i=1,...,4$ are \emph{categorical} variables taking value 0 or 1 with equal probability. The last three variables are standard normal random variables. They are independent of each other. The functions $v_k(\cdot),k=1,...,5$ have the following form: for $k=1,...,5$,
$$
v_k(X) = A_{k1} + A_{k2}X_1 + (A_{k3} + A_{k4}X_2)X_5 + (A_{k5} + A_{k6}X_3)X_6 + (A_{k7} + A_{k8}X_4)X_7.
$$


\textbf{Candidate models.}
The nonconformity score function used here is a greedy scoring rule tailored to the max-min policy from \citet{cortes2024decision}. It is defined as $S_{\lambda}(x,y) = \rho(x,y) + \lambda L(y) $ where $\lambda$ is the score penalty parameter. Candidate models $\{S_\lambda: \lambda \in \Lambda\}$ are established with varying penalty values $\lambda$.

%\textbf{Simulation setting.} 



\textbf{Simulation results.} 
To illustrate the robustness and efficiency of different methods, we consider the effect of labeled sample size $n$ and candidate model size $|\Lambda|$ on decision performance. The simulation results are summarized in Figure \ref{fig:ACSSLP}.
% and {\color{red}nominal robustness level $1-\alpha$ [Haojie: $3\times 3$ is too much, keep two rows.]} on decision performance. The respective simulation results are summarized in Figure \ref{fig:ACSSLP}.


Recall that \texttt{F-CROMS}, \texttt{Naive-CP}, and \texttt{E2E} can achieve finite-sample marginal coverage. We observe that, except for \texttt{E-CROMS}, all methods consistently maintain marginal miscoverage close to the nominal level $\alpha$ across all settings.
However, due to double-dipping bias, \texttt{E-CROMS} yields miscoverage rates exceeding the nominal $\alpha$ when the sample size $n$ is small or the candidate model size $|\Lambda|$ is large, which aligns with the results in Theorem \ref{thm:optimality_ECROMS}. {Notably, the marginal misrobustness of all methods is much less than $\alpha$, meaning the marginal coverage in CRO tends to be relatively conservative.}
As expected, both \texttt{E-CROMS} and \texttt{F-CROMS} outperform baseline methods in terms of averaged decision loss, as they effectively leverage all available data and candidate models for efficient decisions. It is noteworthy that \texttt{F-CROMS} achieves a comparable loss to \texttt{E-CROMS} while still maintaining the coverage guarantee.

