\section{Discussion}
This paper explores optimal model selection for constructing a conformal prediction set in solving CRO problems. We propose two novel frameworks, CROMS and CROiMS, targeting at minimizing average and individual decision risks, respectively. Notably, F-CROMS achieves finite-sample marginal robustness and asymptotic optimality, while E-CROMS relieves computational burden with a diminishing gap in robustness control. CROiMS, an individualized model selection procedure, enjoys asymptotic conditional robustness and conditional optimality. Numerical results demonstrate substantial improvements in decision efficiency and robustness. Our model-free frameworks do not require data splitting, maximizing sample utilization in decision-making.

There are two promising future directions on this topic. First, when $\Lambda$ is a continuous parameter space, implicit differentiation techniques for convex problems \citep{bolte2021nonsmooth} should be employed to minimize the decision risk over the parameters. However, since the corresponding ERM problems are nonconvex in $\lambda$, obtaining a global minimizer remains challenging in practice. Instead of relying on the \texttt{argmin} of the ERM formulation, a more practical robustness and efficiency analysis should be established upon a real algorithm, such as gradient-based bilevel optimization methods \citep{ghadimi2018approximation}. Second, empirical results indicate a persistent gap between \emph{coverage} and \emph{robustness} control, where the standard $1-\alpha$ coverage level for prediction set often leads to a conservative level in decision robustness. A crucial question is how to adaptively adjust the coverage level of prediction sets while maintaining robust decision-making guarantees.

