\subsection{Comparison of the growth rate to existing theoretical framework}\label{sec:growth_rate}
%
We now aim to quantitatively predict the wave growth rate of the waves due to the wind input.
The wind input is used to estimate the instantaneous, non-dimensional wave growth rate, defined as~\citep{belcher1993turbulent,belcher1999wave}
%
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:gamma_inst}
  \gamma(t) = \dfrac{S_{in}(t)}{\omega E_W(t)},
\end{equation}
%
where $S_{in}(t)$ is the wind energy input, $\omega$ the angular frequency, and $E_W(t)$ the wave energy. The non-dimensional wave growth rate, $\gamma(t)$, can be evaluated in an averaged sense, denoted as $\overline{\gamma}$, over the growing stages $G_1$ and $G_2$. The averaged growth rate $\overline{\gamma}$ can then be compared with theoretical frameworks that describe its dependence on wave parameters, particularly the wave slope $\overline{ak}$ and the normalized friction velocity $u_\ast/c$. \par
%
Following~\cite{peirson2008wind,melville2015equilibrium,buckley2020surface}, and relating the wind input to the product of the wave phase speed and the wave-coherent momentum flux, i.e. $S_{in}=c\tau_W$, together with using $E_W=\rho_wga^2/2$ and the dispersion relation $c=\sqrt{g/k}$, the time-averaged, non-dimensional growth rate can be expressed as
%
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:gamma_0}
  %\overline{\gamma} = \tau_W\dfrac{1}{\rho_w c^2}\dfrac{2}{\overline{ak}^2}\mathrm{.}
  \overline{\gamma} = \dfrac{2\tau_W}{\rho_w c^2\overline{ak}^2}\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}
%
Following equation~\eqref{eqn:gamma_0}, finding the non-dimensional growth rate $\overline{\gamma}$ for a wind and wave condition reduces to finding the wave-coherent momentum flux $\tau_W$ defined in equation~\eqref{eqn:tau_W}. Earlier theories summarized in~\cite{kihara2007relationship,wu2022revisiting} based on sheltering~\cite{jeffreys1925formation}, and critical layer~\cite{miles1957generation} lead to $\tau_W=\alpha_p(\overline{ak}^2/2) \rho_a u_\ast^2$, with $\alpha_p$ function of the wind structure and wave slope, or assumed to be a constant, leading to the classic form for $\overline{\gamma}$
%
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:gamma_0}
  \overline{\gamma} = \dfrac{\rho_a}{\rho_w}\alpha_p\left(\dfrac{u_\ast}{c}\right)^2\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}
%
\nsc{In the present configuration, for high values of $u_\ast/c$ and steep waves, the critical layer, i.e. the height where $\langle u \rangle(z) = c$, lies within the viscous sublayer, where the assumptions behind Miles' original theory, particularly the inviscid and quasi-laminar approximations, no longer hold. Although later extensions~\citep {miles1959generation} attempt to incorporate viscous effects, they still rely on the quasi-laminar assumption and neglect wave-induced turbulence, which becomes important for steep waves. \citet{belcher1993turbulent} relaxes these assumptions and accounts for pressure perturbations arising from turbulent sheltering—i.e., asymmetric distortion of turbulent eddies by the wave, which remains effective even when the critical height lies within the roughness or viscous sublayer.
\citet{belcher1993turbulent,belcher1999wave} then theoretically compute the wave-coherent momentum flux $\tau_W$ under such assumptions}. They separate the turbulent boundary layer into an inner and an outer region. In the inner region, which is close to the wave surface, turbulent eddies are assumed in equilibrium with the mean velocity gradient, and the characteristic timescale for the eddy to decorrelate and interact with another one is $T_L=\kappa\eta/u_\ast$. The turbulence flow here can be fairly described with a mixing-length model~\citep{townsend1951structure}. In the outer region, turbulent eddies are rapidly distorted by the flow with a timescale $T_A=k/[\overline{u}(\eta)-c]$, the turbulence flow can be better described using the rapid distortion theory~\citep{belcher1993turbulent}. Under the assumption of negligible flow separation (i.e. non-separated sheltering) over the wave surface,~\citep{belcher1993turbulent} employed formal asymptotic expansions to evaluate the surface pressure and viscous stress distribution. The relations were then employed to directly compute $\tau_{W,p}$ and $\tau_{W,\nu}$ as
%
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:tau_Wp}
  \tau_{W,p}=\dfrac{\alpha_p\overline{ak}^2/2}{1+\alpha_p\overline{ak}^2/2}\rho_au_\ast^2\mathrm{,}
\end{equation}
%
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:tau_Wnu}
  \tau_{W,\nu}=\dfrac{\alpha_\nu\overline{ak}^2/2}{1+\alpha_p\overline{ak}^2/2}\rho_au_\ast^2\mathrm{,}
\end{equation}
%
where the terms $\alpha_p$ and $\alpha_\nu$ are the pre-factors of the pressure and viscous stress momentum fluxes that can be explicitly calculated with knowledge of the wind profile. By substituting equations~\eqref{eqn:tau_Wp} and~\eqref{eqn:tau_Wnu} into~\eqref{eqn:gamma_0}, the non-dimensional wave growth rate can be  expressed as
%
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:gamma_BH}
  \overline{\gamma} = \dfrac{\rho_a}{\rho_w}\dfrac{2(\alpha_p+\alpha_\nu)}{2+\alpha_p\overline{ak}^2}\left(\dfrac{u_\ast}{c}\right)^2\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}
%
Note that eq.~\eqref{eqn:gamma_BH} contains the explicit dependence on the wave steepness $\overline{ak}$. For negligible viscous stress work, i.e. $\alpha_\nu\ll\alpha_p$, and for 'small' $\overline{ak}$, i.e. $\overline{ak}\lesssim 0.15$, $\tau_{W,p}\approx \alpha_p(\overline{ak}^2/2)\rho_a u_\ast^2$, equation~\eqref{eqn:gamma_BH} reduces to eq. (6) from ~\citep{miles1957generation,jeffreys1925formation}. \par
%
The framework developed in~\cite{belcher1999wave} provides a closed expression for $\alpha_p$ and $\alpha_\nu$ which read as follows
%
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:alpha_p}
  \alpha_p = 2\left(\dfrac{\langle u\rangle_m-c}{\langle u\rangle_i-c}\right)^4\left(2-\dfrac{c}{\langle u\rangle_i}\right)-2\left(\dfrac{\langle u\rangle_m-c}{\langle u\rangle_i-c}\right)^2\dfrac{c}{\langle u\rangle_i}+2\kappa\delta^{2n}\left(\dfrac{\langle u\rangle_m-c}{u_\ast}\right)\mathrm{,}
\end{equation}
%
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:alpha_nu}
  \alpha_\nu = \dfrac{2(\langle u\rangle_m-c)^2}{(\langle u\rangle_i-c)u_i}-\dfrac{2c}{\langle u\rangle_i}\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}
%
In equations~\eqref{eqn:alpha_p} and~\eqref{eqn:alpha_nu}, the velocities $\langle u\rangle_m$ and $\langle u\rangle_i$ correspond to the streamwise velocity field evaluated at the two vertical positions in the boundary layers, $z_i$ and $z_m$. Here, $z_i$ is the height in the boundary layer at which $T_A=T_L$, $z_i$ can be computed iteratively from the equation $kz_i|\log(z_i/z_0)-\kappa c/u_\ast|=2\kappa^2$, while $z_m$ is a mid-layer height $kz_m=(\delta/\kappa)^{0.5}$ with $\delta=u_\ast/|u(\lambda/2\pi)|$~\citep{belcher1993turbulent}. It is important to remark that the expressions in equations~\eqref{eqn:alpha_p} and~\eqref{eqn:alpha_nu} reduce the calculation of $\tau_{W}$ and $\overline{\gamma}$ to the evaluation of the streamwise velocity profile at three different heights in the airflow region, i.e. $z=[z_i,z_m,\lambda/2\pi]$. \par
%
Here we extract the pre-factors $\alpha_p$, $\alpha_\nu$ directly from the velocity profiles evaluated from the DNS (profiles are shown in~\cite{scapin2025mom}), shown in Figure~\ref{fig:growth}(b) for different values of $u_\ast/c$. Data point colors indicate different instantaneous $\overline{ak}$ values corresponding to the mean $\overline{ak}$ during the first growing cycle, and from equally sized averaging windows in the second growing cycle, which features two distinct $\overline{ak}$ values. \par
%
We note that when considering a log-layer to describe the mean wind profile $\langle u\rangle=(u_\ast/\kappa)\log(z/z_0)$, the pre-factors $\alpha_p$ and $\alpha_\nu$ can then be evaluated from the values of $u_\ast/c$ and an estimation of the surface roughness $z_0$, as discussed in~\cite{belcher1993turbulent,belcher1999wave}.
For the range of $u_\ast/c$ investigated in this work ($u_\ast/c \in [0.125, 0.9]$), the pre-factors $\alpha_{p,\nu}$ obtained from our DNS exhibit limited sensitivity to variations in $u_\ast/c$, but show a clearer dependence on the surface roughness length $z_0$. In particular, $\alpha_{p,\nu}$ decreases with decreasing $kz_0$, which corresponds to a reduction in the instantaneous steepness $ak$. However, since the range of $kz_0$ covered in our simulations is relatively narrow ($kz_0 \in [4\cdot 10^{-2}, 8\cdot 10^{-2}]$), the resulting variation in $\alpha_{p,\nu}$ remains moderate. Furthermore, the pressure contribution to $\alpha_p$ is significantly larger than the viscous contribution $\alpha_\nu$, with the ratio $\alpha_\nu/\alpha_p$ increasing from approximately $0.04$ at low $u_\ast/c$ to about $0.12$ at the highest $u_\ast/c$ considered. \par
%
Given the limited variability of $\alpha_{p,\nu}$ over the investigated parameter space, it is appropriate to represent them using single mean values in the following. Based on figure~\ref{fig:growth}(b), we adopt $\overline{\alpha}_p = 31.4$ and $\overline{\alpha}_\nu = 4.6$. These values are employed to compute the growth rate using equation~\eqref{eqn:gamma_BH} for different values of steepness in the range reached during the different cases $ak\in [0.15-0.35]$. The results are reported in figure~\ref{fig:growth}(c), where we compare the predictions using~\eqref{eqn:gamma_BH} with the data points extracted from the DNS. Figure~\ref{fig:growth}(c) clearly shows the modulation of $\overline{\gamma}$ induced by wave steepness for a fixed $u_\ast/c$ and emphasizes the importance of its inclusion in the expression of wave-coherent stresses and the growth rate. \par
%
Figure~\ref{fig:growth}(d) combines the estimation of $\overline{\gamma}$ from our DNS, the ones retrieved from~\citep{wu2022revisiting} at lower $u_\ast/c$ and a collection of numerical and experimental data~\citep{wu1979experimental,snyder1981oceanic,plant1982relationship,kihara2007relationship,yang2013dynamic,buckley2020surface}. Furthermore, we include the estimation of the theoretical growth rate following~\eqref{eqn:gamma_BH} (black dot-dashed) with $\overline{\alpha}_p=31.4$, $\overline{\alpha}_\nu=4.6$ for $ak_\mathrm{min}=0.15$, $ak_\mathrm{max}=0.35$, and the one obtained in~\cite{plant1982relationship} (grey dashed lines), who suggested $\gamma=(0.04\pm 0.02)(u_\ast/c)^2$ (equation~\eqref{eqn:gamma_0}). \par
%
As discussed in previous studies, there is a large scatter in the values of $\overline{\gamma}$ at a given $u_\ast/c$, reaching up to one order of magnitude. This can be attributed to several factors. First, the estimation of the wind input $S_{in}$ is subject to significant uncertainty due to the challenges associated with measuring aerodynamic pressure~\citep{grare2013growth}. Second, additional uncertainty stems from the dependence on the wind velocity profile and the lack of direct measurements of such profiles. Reported values of the coefficients, fitted from observed growth rates, i.e. using equation~\eqref{eqn:gamma_0} to retrieve $\alpha_p$, as done in~\cite{peirson2008wind} - vary by up to an order of magnitude~\citep{belcher1999wave}. The framework developed by~\citet{belcher1993turbulent} provides a physical approach to evaluate $\alpha_{p,\nu}$ based on knowledge of the wind profile and highlights the modulation of the growth rate by the wave slope as a source of scatter in the growth rate at a given $u_\ast/c$. In the regime probed here of high values of $u_\ast/c$ and slope $ak$, the critical height becomes located too close to the surface in the surface layer, and a sheltering approach such as the one from Belcher, incorporating the effect of turbulence, becomes preferable.
