		%For a metric (graph) bundle $\pi:X\map B$ such that fibers are uniformly hyperbolic, the barycenter map for fibers are uniformly coarsely surjective and $X$ is hyperbolic, Mj-Sardar (\cite{pranab-mahan}) showed that the Cannon--Thurston (CT) map exists for the inclusion of a fiber into $X$. In this article, we prove that the CT map is surjective if the fibers are one-ended. This result generalizes a theorem of Bowditch and answers a question posed by Lazarovich, Margolis and Mj in \cite{NirMaMj-commen}. Furthermore, we extend this result when the fibers not necessarily one-ended. In particular, surjectivity of the CT map holds when the fibers are uniformly quasiisometric to a fixed nonelementary hyperbolic group.

{\em Metric (graph) bundles} generalize the notion of {\em fiber bundles} to the context of geometric group theory and were introduced by Mj and Sardar in \cite{pranab-mahan}. Suppose $X$ is a metric (graph) bundle over $B$ such that the fibers are ({\em uniformly}) hyperbolic, and the total space $X$ is also hyperbolic. In this generality, Mj--Sardar proved that the inclusion of a fiber into $X$ admits a continuous extension to the (Gromov) boundary.

\noindent In this article, we prove that such a continuous extension map between boundaries is {\em surjective} in the following two key settings.

\begin{enumerate}
\item The fibers are uniformly quasiisometric to a nonelementary hyperbolic group.

\item The fibers are one-ended hyperbolic metric spaces.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent Our result generalizes a theorem of Bowditch in which the fibers were assumed to be the hyperbolic plane, and it answers a question posed by Lazarovich, Margolis and Mj.
