	\section{Introduction}\label{intro}
{\em Metric (graph) bundles} generalize the notion of {\em fiber bundles} to the context of geometric group theory and were introduced by Mj and Sardar (\cite{pranab-mahan}). At the same time, metric (graph) bundles generalize the existing coarse-geometric notion of {\em trees of metric spaces} a la Bestvina--Feighn (\cite{BF,mitra-trees}) in the special case where the inclusions of the edge spaces into the adjacent vertex spaces are uniformly coarsely surjective (quasiisometries). Suppose $X$ is a metric (graph) bundle over $B$ such that the fibers are ({\em uniformly}) hyperbolic, and the total space $X$ is also hyperbolic. In this generality, Mj--Sardar proved that the inclusion of a fiber into $X$ admits a continuous extension to the (Gromov) boundary.\smallskip

\noindent{\em In this article, we prove that such a continuous extension map between boundaries is {\em surjective} in the following two key settings (Corollary \ref{cor-main group over ray}, Theorem \ref{thm-combo1}).

\begin{enumerate}
	\item The fibers are uniformly quasiisometric to a fixed nonelementary hyperbolic group.

	\item The fibers are one-ended hyperbolic metric spaces.
\end{enumerate}}

Suppose $H<G$ are (Gromov) hyperbolic groups. A question posed in \cite[p. $136$]{mitra-ct}, \cite[p. $527$]{mitra-trees} is the following: {\em does the inclusion $i:H\map G$ extend to a continuous map $\pa i:\pa H\map\pa G$}? See also \cite[Question $1.19$]{bestvinaprob}. Such a continuous extension, if it exists, is known as a {\em Cannon--Thurston map} (or {\em CT map} for short) \cite{mitra-ct, mitra-trees} after the pioneering work of Cannon and Thurston (\cite{CT,CTpub}). %, where the first nontrivial example was established.
Their result proves the following. Suppose $M$ is a closed hyperbolic $3$-manifold fibering over a circle with fiber a closed orientable hyperbolic surface $S$. Then the inclusion $\pi_1(S)\map\pi_1(M)$ extends continuously to the boundary (and the extension map between boundaries is surjective). More generally, one may ask the same question in the context of (Gromov) hyperbolic metric spaces $Y\sse X$ (see Definition \ref{CT-map}). %We mention that such an extension is unique if it exists.
%We denote the CT map for the inclusion $i:Y\map X$ by $\pa i:\pa Y\map\pa X$. %We note that the CT map for the inclusion $\pi_1(S)\map\pi_1(M)$ mentioned above is surjective.\smallskip
%\noindent{\em This paper studies the surjectivity of the CT map $\pa i:\pa Y\map\pa X$ when $X$ is equipped with an additional structure -- namely, that of a {\em metric (graph) bundle} (see Questions \ref{qsn-surj in metric bundle} and \ref{main qsn})}.\smallskip

\noindent Generalizing the foundational work of Cannon and Thurston, some positive answers to the above question were given in \cite{mitra-ct, mitra-trees}. Since then, numerous significant results concerning the existence and structure of CT maps have been obtained, particularly in the setting of hyperbolic subgroups of hyperbolic groups -- see, for example, \cite{ps-krishna, ps-kap, mitra-endlam, kl15, dkt, JKLO, baker-riley-hydra, ps-rakesh-ct}. However, the general question posed by Mahan Mitra regarding the existence of CT maps for arbitrary hyperbolic groups was answered negatively in \cite{baker-riley}. Further examples illustrating the nonexistence of CT maps in the hyperbolic group context can be found in \cite{mats-oguni} and \cite{HMS-landing}. For a comprehensive survey of CT maps, we refer the reader to \cite{icm-mj}.

%{\em The main results of this paper are about the surjectivity of CT map in the context of metric (graph) bundles} which were introduced by Mj and Sardar in \cite{pranab-mahan}. Metric (graph) bundles provide a notion of {\em fiber bundles} to geometric group theory. At the same time, they generalize the existing coarse-geometric notion of {\em trees of metric spaces} a la Bestvina--Feighn (\cite{BF}) in the following sense: the bases are arbitrary (hyperbolic) geodesic metric spaces -- not necessarily trees -- while the inclusions of the edge spaces into the adjacent vertex spaces are uniformly coarsely surjective (quasiisometries).
%Metric (graph) bundles are coarse-geometric generalizations of trees of metric spaces a la Bestvina–Feighn (\cite{BF}) in the following sense: the bases are arbitrary (hyperbolic) geodesic metric spaces -- not necessarily trees -- and the inclusions of the edge spaces in the adjacent vertex spaces are uniform coarsely surjective (quasiisometries). %in the special case where the inclusions of the edge spaces in the adjacent vertex spaces are uniform coarsely surjective (quasiisometries). Unlike trees of metric spaces, where the base is a tree, a metric (graph) bundle allows the base to be an arbitrary (hyperbolic) geodesic metric space.
In \cite{bowditch-stacks}, Bowditch studied metric (graph) bundles in a special case, namely when the base is either $\mathbb{R}$ or $\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$, but using a different terminology. He referred to such structures as {\em bi-infinite stacks} and {\em semi-infinite stacks}, respectively. Through this framework, Bowditch established several results concerning Teichm\"uller geodesics and ending laminations in the setting of hyperbolic surfaces. The notion of {\em metric fibrations} were introduced in \cite{farb-mosher} and that can be thought of as metric bundles \cite{pranab-mahan}. In \cite{hamenst-word}, the author used metric fibrations and proved a combination theorem regarding the hyperbolicity of extensions of surface groups. %In \cite{bowditch-stacks}, Bowditch also studied metric (graph) bundles in a very special case and using a different language--namely, {\em bi-infinite stacks}, {\em semi-infinite stacks} mentioned below, and obtained several results on Teichmuller geodesics and ending laminations in the context of closed orientable surface. % One is referred to \cite{farb-mosher} (see also \cite{hamenst-word}) for metric bundles in the language of metric fibrations.


Metric graph bundles (respectively, metric bundles) are formally defined in Definition \ref{defn-metric graph bundle} (respectively, Definition \ref{defn-metric bundle}). Let $\dl\ge0$, $L\ge0$. Suppose $\pi:X\map B$ is a metric (graph) bundle such that:

\begin{itemize}
\item the fibers $F_b=\pi^{-1}(b),~b\in B$ are $\dl$-hyperbolic,

\item the barycenter maps $\pa^3F_b\map F_b$ are $L$-coarsely surjective, i.e., $L$-neighborhood of the image is $F_b$, and

\item the total space $X$ is $\dl$-hyperbolic.
\end{itemize}
Then for any $b\in B$, the inclusion $\pi^{-1}(b)\map X$ admits a CT map $\pa\pi^{-1}(b)\map\pa X$ (\cite[Theorem $5.3$]{pranab-mahan}). In this paper, we address the following question.
%In \cite{pranab-mahan}, the authors proved a combination theorem for metric (graph) bundles. We refer the reader to Definition \ref{defn-metric graph bundle} (respectively, Definition \ref{defn-metric bundle}) for metric graph bundles (respectively, metric bundles). Suppose $\pi: X \to B$ is a metric (graph) bundle such that the base $B$ and the fibers are uniformly hyperbolic, and the barycenter maps for the fibers are uniformly coarsely surjective. Then Mj and Sardar proved that $X$ is hyperbolic, provided that it satisfies the ``Bestvina--Feighn hallway flaring" condition (see Theorem \ref{thm-metric bundle comb}). In this case, authors also proved that for any $b\in B$, the inclusion $i:\pi^{-1}(b)\map X$ admits a CT map $\pa i:\pa\pi^{-1}(b)\map\pa X$.

\begin{question}\label{qsn-surj in metric bundle}
Is the above CT map $\pa\pi^{-1}(b)\map\pa X$ surjective?
\end{question}	

{\em This paper aims to give a positive answer to Question \ref{qsn-surj in metric bundle}.} One of the consequences of our results give an affirmative answer to Question \ref{qsn-surj in metric bundle}  as follows. %in Corollary \ref{cor-main group over ray}. We first introduce the following terminology to state the result precisely and to use it in the introduction later.\smallskip

%\noindent{\bf Uniform bounded valence} (Definition \ref{defn-valence}): {\em A collection of metric graphs $\{\Gamma_{\lambda}:\lambda\in\Lambda\}$ is said to have {\em uniformly bounded valence} if there is a constant $D\ge0$ such that for every $\lambda\in\Lambda$ and for every vertex $u$ of  $\Gamma_{\lambda}$, the valence at $u$ (in $\Gamma_{\lambda}$) is bounded by $D$.}

\begin{cor}\label{cor-main group over ray}
Let $k\ge1$, $D\ge0$. Suppose $\pi:X\map [0,\infty)$ is a metric graph bundle such that:

\noindent$(1)$ the fibers $F_i,~i\in\N\cup\{0\}$ are $k$-quasiisometric to a fixed nonelementary hyperbolic group, and 

\noindent$(2)$ for any vertex $u\in F_i$, the valence at $u$ in $F_i$ is bounded by $D$.

\noindent Further, suppose that $X$ is hyperbolic.

Then the CT map $\pa\pi^{-1}(0)\map\pa X$ is surjective.
\end{cor}

\begin{remark}
