	Since Cayley graphs corresponding to different finite generating sets of a group are quasiisometric to each other, the above corollary implies that the notion of exponential growth is a well-defined property of groups.


	\begin{theorem}\textup{(\cite[Theorem 1.1]{malik-koubi})}\label{thm-exp growth rate hyp grp}
		Suppose $G$ is a nonelementary hyperbolic group. For any finite generating set $S$ of $G$, the Cayley graph of $G$ with respect to $S$ has exponential growth.
		%Then there is $c>1$ depending only $G$ such that for any finite generating set $S$ there is $n_0$ with the following. Let $B(n)$ be the number of elements of $G$ whose world length in $S$ is $\le n$. Then for all $n\ge n_0$, $B(n)>c^n$.
	\end{theorem}

The following result (Theorem \ref{thm-bary imp expo growth}) provides a sufficient condition under which a hyperbolic metric graph (not necessarily a Cayley graph of a hyperbolic group) exhibits exponential growth. We believe that this condition may characterize exponential growth, but we will not pursue that direction here, as it may be of independent interest. (One needs to keep in mind that, in the definition of exponential growth, the associated parameters must be independent of the choice of base point.) %Note that the barycenter map for a nonelementary hyperbolic group is coarsely surjective (see Remark \ref{rmk-bary map coarse surj in gp}).
Note that Theorem \ref{thm-bary imp expo growth} generalizes Theorem \ref{thm-exp growth rate hyp grp} to any (bounded valence) metric graph and it also provides a different approach of proving Theorem \ref{thm-exp growth rate hyp grp}.

	%The following result provides a sufficient condition under which a hyperbolic metric graph (not necessarily a Cayley graph of a hyperbolic group) exhibits exponential growth. While we believe that this condition may also characterize exponential growth (see Question \ref{exp qsn}), we will not explore that here, as it could be of independent interest.
	\begin{theorem}\label{thm-bary imp expo growth}
		Suppose $\Gamma$ is a hyperbolic metric graph and the barycenter map $\pa^3\Gamma\map\Gamma$ is coarsely surjective. Further, suppose that $\Gamma$ has bounded valence. Then $\Gamma$ has exponential growth.

		Moreover, the parameters of exponential growth of $\Gamma$ depend only on the hyperbolicity constant of $\Gamma$ and the coarse surjectivity constant of the barycenter map. %and the number by which valence at each vertex is bounded.
	\end{theorem}

	\begin{proof}
		Let $u\in V(\Gamma)$. Suppose $\Gamma$ is $\dl$-hyperbolic and the barycenter map $\pa^3\Gamma\map\Gamma$ is $L$-coarsely surjective, for some $\dl\ge0$ and $L\ge0$. Let $k=K_{\ref{thm-emb of T3}}(\dl,L)\ge1$ be the constant depending only on $\dl$ and $L$, as appearing in Theorem \ref{thm-emb of T3}. Let $T_3$ denote the trivalent tree with a root vertex $u_0$. 

		Note that $\Gamma$ is a proper metric graph. Then by Theorem \ref{thm-emb of T3}, we have a $k$-qi embedding $\phi:T_3\map\Gamma$ such that $\phi(u_0)=u$. Define \(\phi' : T_3 \to \Gamma\) as follows. For $x\in T_3$,
		\[
		\phi'(x) =
		\begin{cases}
			\phi(x) & \text{if } x \in T_3 \setminus V(T_3), \\
			\phi(x) & \text{if } x \in V(T_3) \text{ and } \phi(x) \in V(\Gamma), \\
			\text{a vertex of the edge containing } \phi(x) & \text{otherwise}.
		\end{cases}
		\]
		%Define $\phi':T_3\map\Gamma$ as follows. $\phi'(x)=\phi(x)$ for all $x\in T_3\setminus V(T_3)$, $\phi'(x)=\phi(x)$ for all $x\in V(T_3)$ and $\phi(x)\in V(\Gamma)$, otherwise $\phi'(x)$ is the one of the vertices of the edge on which $\phi(x)$ lies.
		Since the edges of $\Gamma$ are isometric to the unit interval, by increasing $k$ to $k+2$ if necessary, we may assume that $\phi'$ is also $k$-qi embedding that maps vertices to vertices and $\phi'(u_0)=u$. Then by Example \ref{exmp-T_3 expo} and Lemma \ref{lem-qi implies exponential growth}, we have $a>0$ and $b>1$ depending only on $k$ such that $$||B_{\Gamma}(u,n)||\ge ab^n\text{ for all }n\in\N$$ where $B_{\Gamma}(u,n)$ denotes the $n$-radius ball centered at $u$ in $\Gamma$. Note that $u\in V(\Gamma)$ is arbitrary. Therefore, $\Gamma$ has exponential growth with parameters $(a,b)$ where $a$ and $b$ depend only on $k$, hence, only on $\dl$ and $L$. This completes the proof.
	\end{proof}
