			{\em Proof of subclaim}: There are $x_1\in\Sigma$ and $x_2\in\Sigma'$ such that $d_X(\bt_1(t),x_1)\le D+A(0)$ and $d_X(\bt_2(t),x_2)\le D$ where $D\ge0$ is a constant depending on $\dl$ and $K_1$ (see Lemma \ref{stability of qc}). Without loss of generality, we assume that $\pi(x_2)\le\pi(x_1)$. Now $d_X(x_1,x_2)\le2D+100\dl+A(0)=D_1$ (say), and so $d(\pi(x_1),\pi(x_2))\le D_1$. By taking lifts of the geodesic $[\pi(x_1),\pi(x_2)]$ in $\Sigma$ through $x_1$, we have $y_1\in F_{\pi(x_2)}\cap\Sigma$ such that $d_X(y_1,x_1)\le2kD_1$ (since $\Sigma$ is $k$-qi section). Thus $d_X(y_1,x_2)\le2kD_1+D_1$ and so $d_{\pi(x_1)}(y_1,x_2)\le f(2kD_1+D_1)$ (since fibers are $f$-proper embedding in $X$). This shows that $d(0,\pi(x_1))\le n_kl_1$ for some $l_1$ smallest such that $\lm^{l_1}>f(2kD_1+D_1)$. Note that $l_1$ depends on $D_1$, $k$, $\dl$ and $f$, and hence on $A(0)$. Again by taking lift of the geodesic $[0,\pi(x_1)]$ in $\Sigma'$ through $x_2$, we have $d_X(\Sigma'(0),x_2)\le 2kn_kl_1$. Hence by triangle inequality, we have $t=d_X(\Sigma'(0),\bt_2(t))\le2kn_kl_1+D$. This proofs subclaim.\smallskip

			Therefore, by Lemma \ref{geodesic line}, arclength parametrization of $\bt_1*\bt_2$ is a $K_2$-quasigeodesic line where $K_2\ge1$ is a constant depending on $\dl$ and $2kn_kl+D$, and hence on $A(0)$. Then by Lemma \ref{replace qg}, $\al$ is a $K$-quasigeodesic line where $K$ is a constant depending on $\dl$ and $max\{k,K_2\}$; hence $K$ depends on $\dl$, $k$ and $A(0)$. This completes Claim 2.\smallskip

			Now using Lemma \ref{quasigeodesic div exp}, we will conclude the result. Set $I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}}=[I_{\ref{quasigeodesic div exp}}(\dl,K)]+1$, and fix $x_0\in[\Sigma'(0),\Sigma(0)]_{F_0}$. Then for all $i\in\N$, $d_X(x_0,[\Sigma'(i+I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}}),\Sigma(i+I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}})]_{F_{i+I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}}}})\ge i+I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}}$ (since $\pi$ is $1$-Lipschitz). Note that $\Sigma'(i+I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}}),\Sigma(i+I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}})\in\al$. Therefore, by Lemma \ref{quasigeodesic div exp}, $length~([\Sigma'(i+I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}}),\Sigma(i+I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}})]_{F_{i+I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}}}})=A(i+I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}})\ge b^{i+I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}}-1}$ for all $i\in\N$ where $X$ is $\dl$-hyperbolic and $b=2^{1/\dl}$. Since there are only finitely many numbers less than $I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}}$, we can choose $a>0$ depending on $A(0)$, $\dl$, $k$ and $I_{\ref{once start decreasing is decreasing}}$ such that $A(i)\ge ab^i$ for all $i\in\N\cup\{0\}$.
		\end{proof}
