\subsection{Smoothed fGn for $-1/2<H<0$}
\label{smoothfGn}

Once the smoothed fBm $x_{\Delta}(t)$ is defined, it is interesting to identify the smoothed fGn $\xi_{\Delta}(t)$, which drives this fBm as described by the Langevin equation 
\begin{equation}\label{Langevin10}
\frac{dx_{\Delta}(t)}{dt} = \xi_{\Delta}(t)\,.
\end{equation}
Since  $\xi_{\Delta}(t)$  is a centered stationary  Gaussian process, it is fully determined by its autocorrelation function. The latter  can be 
calculated once the autocorrelation function (\ref{kappaDelta1}) of the smoothed fBm is known. We obtain
\begin{eqnarray}\label{regfGn}
c_{\Delta}(t_1-t_2,H,\Delta) &\equiv& \langle \xi_{\Delta}(t_1) \xi_{\Delta}(t_2) \rangle = \Bigl\langle \frac{d x_{\Delta}(t_1)}{d t_1} \frac{d x_{\Delta}(t_2)}{d t_2}\Bigr\rangle = \frac{\partial^2}{\partial t_1\partial t_2} \kappa_{\Delta}(t_1-t_2) \nonumber \\
&=&\!-\frac{4^H H D \Delta^{2 H-4} \Gamma\left(H+\frac{1}{2}\right)\!\left[(H-1)\tau^2 \,_1F_1\left(2-H;\frac{5}{2};-\frac{\tau^2}{4 \Delta^2}\right)\!+\!3 \Delta ^2 \,_1F_1\left(1-H;\frac{3}{2};-\frac{\tau^2}{4 \Delta^2}\right)\right]}{3 \sqrt{\pi }}\!,
\end{eqnarray}
where $\tau = t_1-t_2$.  Figure \ref{creg} shows a plot of $c_{\Delta}(\tau)$ as a function of $\tau$ for $H=-1/5$ and $\Delta=1/10$. Note that, at $H\to 0$, $c_{\Delta}(\tau)$ vanishes identically for all $\tau$. 


