We also calculated the spectral density (the  Fourier transform of the autocorrelation function) of the smoothed fOU process:
\begin{equation}\label{FTKfOU}
\tilde{\kappa}^{\text{fOU}}_{\omega} =\frac{2 D e^{-\Delta ^2 \omega ^2} \sin (\pi|H|) \,\Gamma (2 H+1) |\omega | ^{1-2 H}}{k^2+\omega ^2}\,.
\end{equation}
The autocorrelation function $\kappa^{\text{fOU}}(\tau)$ itself is given by the inverse Fourier transform,
$\tilde{\kappa}^{\text{fOU}}(\tau) = (2\pi)^{-1} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \tilde{\kappa}^{\text{fOU}}_{\omega} e^{i\omega \tau} d\omega$\,.


\section{Discussion}
\label{discussion}

As demonstrated above, the fractional Brownian motion (fBm), when extended to the strongly anti-persistent regime of  $-1/2<H<0$ and smoothed, exhibits intriguing and nontrivial properties. A similarly interesting behavior characterizes the extended fractional Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (fOU) process. One particularly notable feature of the latter is that the process's variance is independent of the strength of the harmonic confining potential. The extended fBm and fOU processes may prove valuable in modeling a wide range of systems characterized by strongly anti-persistent stationary noise.

An interesting direction for future investigation would be to experimentally test our prediction of complete diffusion arrest -- which can be also called diffusion localization -- within the range $-1/2<H<0$. Such an experiment would require tracking the motion of overdamped particles subjected to smoothed fractional Gaussian noise (fGn) in this strongly anti-persistent regime. Crucially, the externally applied fGn should dominate over thermal noise. Furthermore, it would be fascinating to test the counterintuitive prediction that, under these conditions, the stationary distribution of particle positions becomes nearly independent of the strength of the harmonic potential.
A promising experimental platform for these investigations is colloidal suspensions driven by externally controlled optical forces, such as those employed in the recent study by Roichman's group \cite{Roichman}.


