Before closing this subsection, let us explain how the inward tangent sector provides us with a convenient way to define cusps.

\begin{definition}
    Let $\Omega\subset \ddim$ be open with a non-empty boundary, and $x\in \partial{\Omega}$ be a $\cC^0$ boundary point. We say that $x$ is a cusp if $$int \,  I_x \Omega = \emptyset.$$
\end{definition}

\begin{examples}
    According to the previous definition, the origin $0_3$ is a cusp for both
