\begin{abstract}
  We study the properties of non-topological solitons in two-dimensional conformal field theory. The spectrum of linear perturbations on these solutions is found to be trivial, containing only symmetry-related zero modes. The interpretation of this feature is given by considering the relativistic generalization of our theory in which the conformal symmetry is violated. It is explicitly seen that the restoration of this symmetry leads to the absence of decay/vibrational modes.
\end{abstract}




\maketitle


\section{Introduction} \label{intro}

% conformal symmetry & free Schrodinger

Symmetries play a special role in modern physics. Among many known physical symmetries, one stands out notably: the conformal symmetry. It was previously established that conformal symmetry is related to scale invariance (dilatations), and thus the theory lacks characteristic length scales. In this case, dynamical symmetries of space and time emerge as a symmetry group that is more complex than the common Poincare group, resulting in many interesting features. In the non-relativistic limit the similar happens to the Galilei group as it is completed to the Schr\"{o}dinger group for a free theory \cite{Bargmann:1954gh,PhysRevD.5.377,Niederer:1972zz} (a recent review is given in \cite{Duval:2024eod}).   

% nonlinear theory preserving conformal symmetry

Although interactions typically violate symmetries, there are some special cases in which conformal symmetry is preserved in the non-linear model \cite{deKok:2007ur}. Preservation of an exact scale invariance and conformal symmetry is provided by choosing the specific power of non-linearity in Lagrangian depending on the dimensionality of space.   

%$d$ space dimensions requires interaction term to be of sixth $(d=1)$ or forth.

Non-linear equations of motion may provide soliton solutions that propagate without dispersion and maintain their shape. One of the most elegant examples that can be observed in Nature is the non-topological bright soliton \cite{shabat1972exact}. These objects are often used for the modeling of lumps of Bose condensate. Recently, this approach has been used in various phenomenological models for the description of ultralight dark matter \cite{Hu:2000ke}. In this scope, the dynamics of solitons is of great interest as their excitations provide valuable insights into stability of solutions.    

% Bright solitons. Famous example Gross-Pitaevskii, Zakharov-Shabat. Linear stability (Kaup?)

% In this paper, we provide a discussion of properties of bright solitons (integral characteristics, linear stability).

In this paper, we study non-relativistic bright solitons and their properties within the framework of two-dimensional conformal field theory. For our model, we have found a continuous branch of analytical solutions of different sizes that represent bright solitons. Remarkably, their properties: energy, $U(1)$ charge and linear stability do not depend on the width. In order to examine the role of conformal symmetry in these processes, we study how this symmetry is restored in the relativistic model. In this generalized model, we have found a decay mode that is in agreement with the Vakhitov-Kolokolov criterion \cite{Vakhitov:1973lcn}.   

% Contents.

We introduce a two-dimensional interacting model that preserves dilatations and conformal symmetry in Sec.\ref{sec.1}. A brief overview of the symmetries of our model is also provided. An explicit discussion of conformal bright solitons and their properties is given in Sec.\ref{sec.2}. The aspects of relativistic generalization and conformal symmetry restoration are discussed in Sec.\ref{sec.3}.    

\section{The model and symmetries}\label{sec.1}

In this section, we introduce non-relativistic Lagrangian\footnote[1]{Bearing in mind following relativistic generalization, we use natural system of units $\hbar=c=1$.} of complex scalar field

\begin{equation} \label{NR lagrangian}
    \mathcal{L}_{GP6} = i\psi^{\ast}\dot{\psi} - \frac{1}{2m}\nabla\psi^{\ast}\nabla\psi + \frac{\lambda}{24m^{3}}\left(\psi^{\ast}\psi \right)^{3}
\end{equation}
of our model in one space dimension. The corresponding equation of motion is a quintic non-linear Schr\"{o}dinger equation (NLSE), which is also known as the Gross-Pitaevskii equation with the quintic term   

\begin{equation}\label{NLSE}
    i\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\psi = \left[-\frac{\nabla^{2}}{2m} - \frac{\lambda}{8m^{3}}\left( \psi^{\ast}\psi \right)^{2} \right]\psi.
\end{equation}

Physically, the quintic term may simulate three-body interactions and/or deviations of the dimensionality of the condensate  (see  e.g.\cite{gammal2000atomic,Avelar:2008va,kengne2008bose}).

Both Eq.(\ref{NLSE}) and the Lagrangian (\ref{NR lagrangian}) possess a global $U(1)$ symmetry along with a Schr\"{o}dinger group invariance (see \cite{deKok:2007ur} and references therein). An internal global $U(1)$ symmetry is responsible for the conservation of a particle number

\begin{equation}\label{particle number N}
    N = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} dx \psi^{\ast}(t,x)\psi(t,x).
\end{equation}

